WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.190
- Good evening everyone,
I'm Priya Natarajan.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:11.630
I'm the current director of the

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.510
Franke Program in Science
and the Humanities.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:17.130
And I'm an astrophysicist by training

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:20.990
and I'm a faculty member at
Yales Departments of Physics

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:23.020
and the Department of Astronomy.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:24.680
So I am delighted

00:00:25.615 --> 00:00:28.900
to welcome you all to this evening's talk

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:33.496
which is part of our
ongoing thematic workshop

00:00:33.496 --> 00:00:37.380
and study on the nature of inference,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.100
understanding the nature of inference,

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.330
in particular, understanding
causation, correlation,

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:44.960
and understanding these issues

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.750
from a multidisciplinary viewpoint.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:51.167
So as many of you know, we've
had these monthly seminars,

00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:53.850
we've had experts, pioneers

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.620
who have been working on
using machine learning

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.190
and artificial intelligence methodologies

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:02.860
in their particular disciplines,
ranging from chemistry,

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:07.360
biology, computer science,
and physics today.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.960
And I am really happy to
introduce today's speaker

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:15.789
who's part of our core team
for this project as well.

00:01:15.789 --> 00:01:19.650
And today's speaker is
Professor Kyle Cranmer,

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.560
who is currently at NYU,

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:26.560
but before I give you an
introduction to Kyle and his work,

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:30.243
I want to say a big thank
you to our benefactors,

00:01:31.136 --> 00:01:33.340
Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.930
who I think are amongst
the audience today.

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:37.700
Their generosity and support

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.950
of all the interdisciplinary
activities at Yale,

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:44.380
multifarious activities and
centers that they support,

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:48.520
including the Franke Program
in Science and the Humanities,

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.930
has been very, very
impactful in getting scholars

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:55.330
who are otherwise in silos to
actually collaborate, talk,

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:56.780
and interact with each other.

00:02:00.293 --> 00:02:02.970
So let me go on now to
tell you a little bit more

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.570
about our wonderful speaker.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:05.980
Kyle is currently a professor

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.780
of physics and data science at NYU,

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:10.833
and he's a visiting scientist at Meta.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.430
In July, he will become the director

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:19.479
for the American Family
Insurance Data Science Institute

00:02:19.479 --> 00:02:21.780
at the University of Wisconsin, Madison.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:26.780
It's their brand new effort
in data science at Madison.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.710
He obtained his PhD in physics

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.710
from the University of Wisconsin,

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:34.280
so he's going back home, in a way, in 2005

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.600
and he got his BA in
mathematics and physics

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.470
from Rice University.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:40.220
He's a recipient of many awards.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:42.340
I'll just list a few.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:44.770
He was awarded the
Presidential Early Career Award

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:48.780
for Science and Engineering,
the PECASE, in 2007,

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.610
the National Science Foundations
Career Award in 2009,

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:55.480
and he became a fellow of
the American Physical Society

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.970
for his work on the Large Hadron Collider.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:02.450
So one of the key, sort of
the seminal contributions

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:07.450
that Kyle has made is the
development of a framework,

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:09.430
a very robust framework

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:12.360
that enables collaborative
statistical modeling.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.770
And as you can imagine,

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:17.310
with the vast amount of data
from the Large Hadron Collider,

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:20.650
his work was used
extensively for the discovery

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:23.430
of the Higgs boson, which as you all know,

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:25.430
was a major breakthrough
in particle physics

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:27.960
announced in 2012 at CERN.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.430
So his current interests are
at the intersection of physics,

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:33.814
statistics, and machine learning.

00:03:33.814 --> 00:03:38.120
And as you can probably guess,
he's one of the pioneers

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:42.524
in our field trying to leverage
these new innovative methods

00:03:42.524 --> 00:03:47.524
for obtaining a deeper
understanding of the physical world.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.600
So before I turn it over to him,

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.880
let me very quickly remind you

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:54.670
that the format of these talks,

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:59.670
please feel free to put
your questions in the chat.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.970
You have a burning question,
do raise your hand,

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:05.980
something that requires
immediate clarification.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:10.290
Otherwise we prefer to have
the Q&amp;A session after the talk.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:11.470
And as you all know,

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:14.670
the format of these talks has been,

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:16.970
we first start out with a colloquium,

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:18.530
and so which Kyle will be giving,

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:21.840
followed by a few days later,

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:25.070
an actual sort of discussion session

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:27.040
in which we have an interlocutor,

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.480
another expert in the
field who approaches things

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:31.450
from a slightly different vantage point,

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:34.550
engaging with Kyle's talk today,

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:37.780
as well as offering us their own insights.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:41.040
And so we are delighted
to have Dr. Zenna Tavares

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.250
who's at Columbia, who is also
here in the audience today,

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.830
who will be leading the discussion session

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:49.720
and framing some of the open questions

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:51.680
and engaging with the material

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:55.040
that Kyle will be presenting today.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:58.666
And these sessions have been
wonderful lively discussions

00:04:58.666 --> 00:05:01.060
and really look forward to it.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.760
So Zenna and Kyle will be in conversation

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:07.200
next Wednesday at 4:00 PM.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.800
And to quickly give you a
preview of coming attractions,

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.120
we have Carl Zimmer who will be speaking

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.450
as part of the "Distinguished
Speaker Series"

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.700
about his new book, "Life's Edge,"

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:20.610
and that's at the end
of March, March 30th.

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:23.430
And then we have our "Mapping
As Knowing" lecture series

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:26.580
continuing with Victor Coello in April.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.340
And we have a bunch of our
own Franke fellow speaking

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:30.720
later on in April.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.798
Anyway, without further ado,

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:34.970
let me now turn it over to Kyle.

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:36.900
Kyle, we are delighted.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:39.760
I wish we could have hosted you in person,

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:43.087
both Kyle and Zenna, but
things are as they are.

00:05:43.087 --> 00:05:48.087
I think we are just really
grateful and deeply appreciative

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:52.878
of you agreeing to speak
to us on Zoom, thank you.

00:05:52.878 --> 00:05:54.410
- Thank you very much, Priya,

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:56.330
for the warm introduction
and thank you, Zenna,

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:58.760
for agreeing to the
conversation next week.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:00.860
I'm really looking forward to it.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.653
Let me share my screen here,
hopefully this is fine.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.700
Okay, does that look good?

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:07.870
- Yep, looks great.
- Great.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:12.203
- Gonna hide you so I can see
a little bit better, okay.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:18.150
So yeah, I'm really excited
to be invited in this series.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:19.150
It's been really interesting.

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:22.050
I've been able to watch
quite a few of the talks,

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:24.620
not all of them, but I think most of them,

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:27.280
as well as the discussions
and conversations

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:30.190
that have followed, which I
find to be really interesting.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:31.280
I like the format a lot.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.408
So thank you very much for having me.

00:06:33.408 --> 00:06:37.660
I think I'm maybe the last
one of this particular slate,

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:40.510
but maybe they'll be added some more.

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:42.610
So I tried to take on
a little bit of a role

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:44.620
of referring back to
some of the other talks

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:46.650
that happened in the series.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:48.991
And so the title I chose is

00:06:48.991 --> 00:06:51.914
Causality at the
Intersection of Simulation,

00:06:51.914 --> 00:06:54.160
Inference, Science, and Learning,

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:55.340
and hopefully be able to see

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:57.143
how all these get tied together.

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:00.310
So I'm gonna start by
saying that there were a few

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:02.420
of the talks that happened earlier

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:06.060
where causality was being examined

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:07.890
and that word was being used

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:10.943
in a particular way
that physicists use it,

00:07:10.943 --> 00:07:13.040
which has to do, for instance,

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:15.020
with things like the arrow of time,

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:16.680
the structure of space time.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:18.243
That's my little diagram here.

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:22.590
This is some of, whoops,
excuse me, some event

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:25.640
and you have a future light
cone and a pass light cone,

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:28.607
and the notion of two
events being maybe separated

00:07:28.607 --> 00:07:30.990
so that they're out of causal
contact with each other

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:34.940
and other points that are in
causal contact with each other,

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:37.293
trying to understand
that in the context of,

00:07:38.399 --> 00:07:40.530
like I mentioned before, the arrow of time

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:43.850
or in the context of quantum
mechanics, et cetera.

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:45.963
And these were very interesting talks,

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:48.100
but there were some other talks

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:52.330
that were a slightly different
usage of the word causation,

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:55.340
which is more in the language
of sort of causal inference

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:58.200
and kind of from the
statistical point of view.

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.430
So there you have things
like here's my little picture

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:05.430
of a sun and the sun is
leading to hot sunny weather,

00:08:05.595 --> 00:08:10.595
which kind of causes people
eating ice cream and sunburns.

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:13.710
And then that gives rise
to some kind of correlation

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:15.200
between ice cream and sunburns.

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:17.140
But we would probably all agree to say

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:19.930
it's wrong to say that
ice cream causes sunburns.

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:23.463
And so that's the typical correlation,

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:26.980
correlation, causation
aren't the same thing.

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:29.850
And so the mathematical framework

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:30.807
for trying to think about this

00:08:30.807 --> 00:08:33.370
and causal reasoning was
sort of the subject of,

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:35.223
for instance, these two talks.

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:39.570
Now, after the talk by Jenann Ismael,

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:43.860
James Woodward was the
conversant that followed up.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:46.330
And I grabbed a little
bit of the transcript

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:48.880
from their conversation 'cause I found it

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.840
to be very interesting and
it's kind of a nice entry point

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.290
into what I wanted to talk about today.

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:56.390
So James was pointing out
in response to the talk

00:08:56.390 --> 00:09:01.260
that there were potentially
some interesting route

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:03.520
to be had on the philosophical side

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:06.850
by thinking about what he talked about,

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:10.550
a component that has a
more normative character.

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:13.513
Talked about the procedures
that make causal reasoning

00:09:13.513 --> 00:09:16.010
or learning causal relationships

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:18.290
from certain kinds of data reliable.

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:21.726
And he said that these sort of
normative aspects of things,

00:09:21.726 --> 00:09:23.740
when you do that, then you
bring in the connection

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:27.250
with statistics, causal reasoning,
machine learning, and AI,

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:29.870
and that it would be
interesting to sort of see

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:31.500
how these two ways of going

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:34.080
about the general topic
relate to each other.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:36.760
And so I'm going to
definitely sort of follow up

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:37.910
from that a little bit.

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:41.400
And so, while I am,

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:43.590
my core discipline as a physicist,

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:45.690
I've been doing a lot of work
on machine learning and AI.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:48.850
And a lot of what I have
to say kind of comes more

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:50.230
on the statistics side of things,

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:52.770
but they're of course very deeply related.

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:55.120
And so to try to kind of prime that,

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:57.650
just give a few graphic examples.

00:09:57.650 --> 00:10:00.020
So here's a system.

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:03.020
It's a toy that I have
on my desk right now.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:04.830
And when you see this kind of thing,

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:06.240
you see the balls bouncing around,

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:09.640
it sort of naturally draws to mind

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:11.820
a probabilistic description
of what's going on.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:14.150
And of course, thinking about these kinds

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:17.300
of things are really
back in the early history

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:21.280
of our statistics and probability.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:23.350
But if you think about
the system as it evolves,

00:10:23.350 --> 00:10:24.850
when you describe it,

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:26.560
it's natural to think of
the balls bouncing either

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:29.300
to the left or to the right
with some probability.

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:32.260
And so you have this
probabilistic description

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:34.663
where the system evolves
in a stochastic way.

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:39.160
And now the same system, if
you look at it more closely

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:42.650
and in slow motion, you see
the balls bouncing around.

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:45.160
And now you're thinking maybe
more like classical mechanics

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:48.080
of a deterministic evolution of a system

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:52.960
where the balls are bouncing
through this lattice of pins.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:55.390
And if you knew the
initial state perfectly,

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:59.340
you know it would unfold in
a totally deterministic way.

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:02.080
And so that kind of points
to this other notion

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:05.100
of causation that's more from
the physicist's point of view

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:07.510
and things like the arrow
of time and et cetera.

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:09.440
Now, if you want to try
to sort of reconcile

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:11.980
these two different ways of
looking at the same system,

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:15.070
we know that this is very sensitive

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:16.110
to the initial conditions.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:18.420
And if you slightly perturbed the balls

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:20.500
at the very beginning, they
would take a different path

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:23.050
through the lattice of pins.

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:25.260
And so that, so each realization of

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:29.010
turning this desk toy over gives you

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:31.293
effectively some random outcome.

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:34.560
Now, this also happens
when you, for instance,

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:36.630
look at climate science.

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:38.580
These are simulations that are going on

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:42.313
of eddies inside of the oceans.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:46.160
The description of this
is fluid mechanics.

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:48.600
So you have things like
Navier-Stokes equations,

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:50.810
again, that are deterministic.

00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:52.830
There is some scale at
which you can't resolve

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:54.370
what's going on anymore.

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:57.100
And so you have this
kind of chaotic behavior.

00:11:57.100 --> 00:11:59.280
And so there is a connection again to

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:02.633
randomness and stochasticity.

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:04.920
What is kind of interesting here,

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:07.580
the higher level picture is kind of looks

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:09.590
like deterministic and then,

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:12.620
but then you have some
lower level information

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:13.453
that you can't resolve,

00:12:13.453 --> 00:12:16.618
so that introduces some
randomness into the system.

00:12:16.618 --> 00:12:19.970
And then you have different
arrangements of this

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:21.360
and I guess there's just the observation

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:24.140
that as you sort of step through,

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:25.660
step down the reductionist ladder

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:30.660
or maybe step up the ladder
of emergent phenomena,

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:33.650
that you often see switches
in whether or not the kind

00:12:33.650 --> 00:12:35.760
of effective description
that you're working with

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:37.660
looks stochastic or deterministic

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:40.490
and I think that's just kind
of an interesting observation.

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:42.810
So for instance, when you're
talking about thermodynamics

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:45.860
or thermostatics, you
have pressure and volume

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:47.790
and these kinds of quantities,

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:49.640
and there are equations that relate them

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:51.550
and it looks much more deterministic.

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:55.780
But we then understand
thermodynamics is arising

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:59.690
from an underlying statistical
mechanical description

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:01.140
of what's going on.

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:04.000
And then here, kind of
flipping the roles around,

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:05.920
this is supposed to be
the two-slit experiment.

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:08.683
And at the top, you see, for instance,

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:12.560
electrons hitting some screen
or something like that,

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:16.240
and the individual electrons
are like point-like particles,

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:18.700
and they have a random
nature that's associated

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:20.920
with the kind of randomness
of quantum mechanics.

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:22.670
But at the same time, we think of a kind

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:25.170
of underlying deterministic evolution

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:28.090
of the wave function due
to Schrodinger's equation.

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:30.040
So this is just basically to prime

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:31.610
that the two different ways of thinking

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:33.840
about causality and deterministic systems

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:37.970
and stochastic systems
are very closely related.

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:42.170
I'm going to be talking more
about stochastic systems,

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:46.250
but just keep in mind this relationship.

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:48.790
Now in the conversation
that I alluded to before,

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:53.280
later on about a half an
hour into the discussion,

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:56.030
James pointed out again
that this important

00:13:57.120 --> 00:13:59.110
topic called scale separation,

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:01.660
which is essentially the
observation that if you wanted

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:03.750
to describe a bulldozer, you don't need

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:07.982
to bring in quantum mechanics
or quantum chromodynamics

00:14:07.982 --> 00:14:09.810
to try to describe that.

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:12.210
And he says you could
imagine an alternative world

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:13.610
where to basically model anything,

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:16.220
you have to model all the way down

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:18.913
to the most detailed way.

00:14:20.044 --> 00:14:22.010
But that doesn't seem how to be like

00:14:22.010 --> 00:14:25.230
how our world is arranged and
that we often see some kind

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:27.910
of approximate decoupling
between the different scales

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:30.670
and that facilitates causal
reasoning in our world.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:32.980
And I 100% agree with that,

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:35.593
and I'm gonna sort of show
some examples of that.

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:40.060
So now moving on to the kind of,

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:42.340
the topic of the series as inference,

00:14:42.340 --> 00:14:46.170
and of course it's related
to causation and correlation.

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:49.330
And so the way that I'm going
to be talking about it is

00:14:49.330 --> 00:14:52.430
that in the context of
some scientific theory,

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:55.300
for instance, as a particle
physicist, we have theories

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:58.060
and those theories often
have some parameters of,

00:14:58.060 --> 00:15:00.270
call those theta, and
then I can use that theory

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:02.120
to make predictions about what I will see

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:03.843
in a particular experiment.

00:15:05.005 --> 00:15:07.062
And so I'm making
predictions about the data,

00:15:07.062 --> 00:15:09.140
which I call x here.

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:12.210
And the process of inference
is sort of the inverse of that.

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:14.630
From some data I would
like to infer something

00:15:14.630 --> 00:15:17.573
about the theory that's in question.

00:15:19.030 --> 00:15:22.120
And that can be done in
various different ways.

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:24.660
You know, often it's
phrased in the context

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:27.853
of Bayesian inference where
if you wanna say something

00:15:27.853 --> 00:15:31.570
probabilistic about a
theory, given some data,

00:15:31.570 --> 00:15:33.150
via Bayes theorem, you can relate that

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:35.120
to a statement about the probability

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:36.690
of the data, given the theory,

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:39.140
so this is what's called
the likelihood function,

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:43.580
and then times a prior
probability for the theory.

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:45.860
Now in particle physics, I'll
just make a quick comment

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:47.760
that the prior probability for the theory

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:50.500
is a very thorny, thorny thing.

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:52.940
It's not like what's the prior probability

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:57.430
that you have the flu
or something before you,

00:15:57.430 --> 00:16:00.510
or you have COVID before
you take a COVID test.

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:01.820
Here, you're talking about like,

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:04.940
does some fundamental particle
exist in the universe or not?

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:07.620
And it's hard to talk about
that in a probabilistic way,

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:11.743
other than through more like
subjective notions of belief.

00:16:12.650 --> 00:16:15.580
But this term, the likelihood
function is sort of

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:20.135
very well-grounded and how we
as physicists take a theory

00:16:20.135 --> 00:16:22.040
and turn that into predictions about

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:23.950
what we expect the data to look like.

00:16:23.950 --> 00:16:26.120
And I'm not going to get into the issues

00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:29.320
of Bayesian versus frequentist
statistics, really,

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:31.750
other than just to
point out it's an issue.

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:34.630
The main thing I'm gonna
be you focusing on here

00:16:34.630 --> 00:16:37.390
is the details and difficulties
with the probability

00:16:37.390 --> 00:16:40.653
of the data, given the theory
or the likelihood function.

00:16:40.653 --> 00:16:42.690
And so I'll come back to that later,

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:45.060
but this is sort of roughly
the probabilistic way

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:47.383
in which I'll be discussing inference.

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:52.440
Now, this is a picture
of an actual collision

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:57.040
that happened in 2012
and on the 10th of June

00:16:57.040 --> 00:16:58.440
at the Large Hadron Collider

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:00.980
in the Atlas Experiment that I work on.

00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:04.270
And obviously it's a computer rendering

00:17:04.270 --> 00:17:07.410
of an actual collision
that happened in the data.

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:08.900
I'll talk about it a little bit

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:11.330
in some more detail in a moment.

00:17:11.330 --> 00:17:15.150
This was one of approximately
a quadrillion collisions

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:16.160
at the Large Hadron Collider

00:17:16.160 --> 00:17:19.240
that happened in those couple years.

00:17:19.240 --> 00:17:21.580
So there were many, many
collisions like this,

00:17:21.580 --> 00:17:24.330
but this one was one that
was particularly important

00:17:24.330 --> 00:17:27.040
because it was a candidate
for being consistent

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:28.990
with the production of the Higgs boson.

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:32.620
So we had more collisions
that were like that

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:35.230
and we did some statistical
analysis of the data,

00:17:35.230 --> 00:17:37.247
which I'll say a little bit more about,

00:17:37.247 --> 00:17:39.540
and that's what led to the claim

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:42.630
of the discovery in July of 2012.

00:17:42.630 --> 00:17:44.660
And one of the things that's
interesting about this is this,

00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:47.270
really within about a week of that,

00:17:47.270 --> 00:17:50.610
was one of the kind of
breakthrough milestone papers

00:17:50.610 --> 00:17:51.860
in deep learning.

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:54.860
And that's when we really
started seeing deep learning

00:17:54.860 --> 00:17:58.770
as we think of it today in
the context of image analysis,

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:02.510
image classification, really
starting to work quite well.

00:18:02.510 --> 00:18:04.800
And very shortly after this,

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:06.280
you started getting situations

00:18:06.280 --> 00:18:10.413
where deep neural networks
were sort of claims

00:18:10.413 --> 00:18:12.675
of superhuman performance
and things like this.

00:18:12.675 --> 00:18:16.060
So, this was definitely
a mile milestone moment,

00:18:16.060 --> 00:18:17.860
both from the point of
view of deep learning

00:18:17.860 --> 00:18:19.440
and from particle physics.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:21.790
And for me personally, right after this,

00:18:21.790 --> 00:18:23.370
I went on sabbatical and that's kind of,

00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:27.030
I really shifted my
focus to thinking about

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:28.310
how to put these two together.

00:18:28.310 --> 00:18:31.360
How can we take advantage
of what's going on

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:33.220
in machine learning and deep learning

00:18:33.220 --> 00:18:35.430
for the purposes of doing science,

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:38.190
and what does that relationship look like?

00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:41.460
And this was interesting because
it's kind of the backdrop

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:45.880
of a debate that's happening
across all the sciences,

00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:47.860
where you see a pendulum swinging,

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:50.030
essentially from, in
the context of physics,

00:18:50.030 --> 00:18:52.900
you had traditional approaches
to analyzing the data

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:56.270
where you have more handcrafted
types of data analysis

00:18:56.270 --> 00:19:00.100
largely guided by expert
knowledge and theoretical insight.

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:03.224
Switching over to something
that's really started,

00:19:03.224 --> 00:19:07.414
even a decade or more before
with the rise of big data

00:19:07.414 --> 00:19:09.760
and data science and deep learning.

00:19:09.760 --> 00:19:13.963
And there's a push to be
ever more data-driven,

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:16.520
in the context of machine learning,

00:19:16.520 --> 00:19:18.697
you use this phrase end-to-end learning.

00:19:18.697 --> 00:19:20.930
And that basically is hinting at sort of

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:22.510
a shooing expert knowledge

00:19:23.790 --> 00:19:28.460
and what's sometimes referred
to as hand-designed features

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:29.600
and things like this, and really try

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:32.850
to have an end-to-end system driven by

00:19:32.850 --> 00:19:35.360
with some deep neural network
or something in the middle

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:37.000
that tries to go from the data directly

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:38.100
to solving your problem.

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:41.390
So that would be something
like data from the LHC to, yes,

00:19:41.390 --> 00:19:44.370
you discovered the Higgs
or not like in one step.

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:46.580
And so that's a very different approach,

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:48.670
and this is paired with the kind of desire

00:19:48.670 --> 00:19:51.590
that maybe AI is somehow like magical

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:52.900
and it's just gonna look at all the data

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:54.500
and figure things out.

00:19:54.500 --> 00:19:56.950
And it's, again, paired with a discussion

00:19:56.950 --> 00:19:58.840
that started really with big data

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:00.930
about these kinds of, I would say,

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:02.976
somewhat ridiculous claims

00:20:02.976 --> 00:20:04.857
that we're looking at, "the
end of theory," and that,

00:20:04.857 --> 00:20:08.560
"the data deluge makes the
scientific method obsolete."

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:11.407
Quotes like this, that,
"All models are wrong

00:20:11.407 --> 00:20:13.857
"and increasingly you can
succeed without them."

00:20:15.590 --> 00:20:17.410
And you're given
everything that's going on,

00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:19.190
it's hard to just dismiss these claims

00:20:19.190 --> 00:20:20.660
because something is happening.

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:23.220
But so that's partially
what we wanna look into

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:27.840
a little bit more deeply
is to what degree is this,

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:30.350
are we witnessing some
kind of radical change

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:34.143
in the scientific method and
what does that look like?

00:20:35.323 --> 00:20:37.830
So I'm gonna switch now to kind of

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:42.280
contrast what I was just
saying on the big data side

00:20:42.280 --> 00:20:45.320
with the go back to the
more traditional approach,

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:48.630
and then start to bring
in elements of causation.

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:52.660
And I'll also say that while
physicists are very comfortable

00:20:52.660 --> 00:20:57.000
with talking about causation
in the sense of space and time

00:20:58.573 --> 00:21:00.710
and these kinds of things,

00:21:00.710 --> 00:21:03.330
oftentimes we are not
particularly well trained

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:07.130
in the language of causal
inference and things like this.

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:09.330
It's actually something that's
very core, for instance,

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:12.540
in the social sciences
and in medical sciences

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:15.860
and sometimes in particle physics
or other areas of physics.

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:18.400
The causal relationship is so obvious

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:19.900
that it's not really
something that you needed

00:21:19.900 --> 00:21:23.080
to develop a sort of
calculus to understand.

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:25.690
And so in that way, we're playing catch-up

00:21:25.690 --> 00:21:27.810
but at the same time, a lot of the success

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:30.050
of physicists has been based

00:21:30.050 --> 00:21:33.300
on leveraging causality,
notions of causality.

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:34.750
And so that's a kind of interesting thing

00:21:34.750 --> 00:21:35.650
to try to reconcile.

00:21:35.650 --> 00:21:38.730
It's partially what I'm,
thinking about a lot these days.

00:21:38.730 --> 00:21:41.390
So this is a picture
with me and Judea Pearl

00:21:41.390 --> 00:21:44.610
who wrote this "Book of
Why," and one of the,

00:21:44.610 --> 00:21:47.170
in his book and in his writings,

00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:50.640
he talks about what he calls
the latter of causation.

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:52.260
And he's saying the lowest rung

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:55.700
of the ladder is association.

00:21:55.700 --> 00:21:58.680
So that's really at the
level of like correlations.

00:21:58.680 --> 00:22:01.950
And so what you see is sort
of seeing and observing

00:22:03.103 --> 00:22:04.700
where you just have data, basically.

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:08.390
And the point here is that
there are a set of questions

00:22:08.390 --> 00:22:11.150
that you can address when
you have a lot of data,

00:22:11.150 --> 00:22:12.770
but there are other types of questions

00:22:12.770 --> 00:22:15.500
that you cannot address just with data.

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:18.270
So the idea that the big
data is gonna solve all

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:20.610
your problems is really only restricted

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:22.880
to this lowest rung on the ladder.

00:22:22.880 --> 00:22:25.690
And in fact, there are
higher-level questions

00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:27.637
where you want to ask questions like,

00:22:27.637 --> 00:22:30.050
"What would happen if I do the following,"

00:22:30.050 --> 00:22:31.923
if you intervene on the system.

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:36.330
And those kinds of
questions cannot be answered

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:37.820
just through data alone.

00:22:37.820 --> 00:22:40.870
They really require
essentially some other kind

00:22:40.870 --> 00:22:42.020
of structure that you add to it,

00:22:42.020 --> 00:22:44.140
usually in the form of
some kind of assumption,

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:47.343
and I will give a concrete
example in just a moment.

00:22:48.350 --> 00:22:49.890
But before doing so, I'll just say

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:51.860
that there's even a
higher level of question

00:22:51.860 --> 00:22:54.410
in which are referred
to as counterfactuals,

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:55.510
where you're imagining

00:22:56.369 --> 00:22:58.207
and you're sort of
answering questions like,

00:22:58.207 --> 00:23:00.977
"What would have happened if
I had done the following?"

00:23:02.099 --> 00:23:03.470
And so that's interesting

00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:05.750
because you're sort of conditioning

00:23:05.750 --> 00:23:07.060
on things that actually happen.

00:23:07.060 --> 00:23:09.183
So you kind of, in principle,
can't answer that question

00:23:09.183 --> 00:23:10.880
because it already happened,

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:12.490
while in the context of interventions,

00:23:12.490 --> 00:23:14.110
you could make the interventions

00:23:14.110 --> 00:23:15.960
and actually see what happens, right?

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:18.120
So these are the three levels

00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:22.600
and they do have a very grounded,
formal mathematical basis,

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:24.400
which I'm not gonna go
into, but just to say,

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:26.210
they're not squishy concepts.

00:23:26.210 --> 00:23:30.950
They're really very formalized,
well-defined concepts.

00:23:30.950 --> 00:23:33.760
So let me give a concrete example.

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:36.540
This is, here you see some code.

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:39.487
As I said, there's three very
short little computer programs

00:23:39.487 --> 00:23:42.470
where you have random variables, x and y,

00:23:42.470 --> 00:23:44.910
and here you also have
a random variable z.

00:23:44.910 --> 00:23:47.370
And then there's a little
drawing that's associated to it.

00:23:47.370 --> 00:23:51.470
So here, the story is that the
random variable x was first,

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:53.410
and then it sort of causes y.

00:23:53.410 --> 00:23:56.520
And you see, in the
program, x was generated

00:23:56.520 --> 00:23:58.750
through the random number generator first,

00:23:58.750 --> 00:24:00.320
and then y depends on x.

00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:02.853
So this is the direction of causation.

00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:06.550
Here, you see the opposite,
y is generated first

00:24:06.550 --> 00:24:09.510
and then x is generated
and it's a function of y.

00:24:09.510 --> 00:24:12.403
And then finally you have a
different situation where z,

00:24:14.300 --> 00:24:17.480
z is a third variable that's generated

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:20.290
and then y and x both depend on z.

00:24:20.290 --> 00:24:22.990
Okay, fine, so, and
here's the little drawing

00:24:22.990 --> 00:24:24.380
associated to that.

00:24:24.380 --> 00:24:26.780
Now, why is this interesting?

00:24:26.780 --> 00:24:27.833
The reason that this
is interesting is here

00:24:27.833 --> 00:24:29.860
there's three different stories about

00:24:29.860 --> 00:24:30.990
how the data came to be,

00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:33.720
the data generating process, but all three

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:37.530
of them give you exactly
the same joint distribution,

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:40.410
or the correlations between
x and y are exactly the same

00:24:40.410 --> 00:24:42.060
in these three different stories.

00:24:43.010 --> 00:24:45.110
So in that sense, if you just had data,

00:24:45.110 --> 00:24:46.470
you wouldn't able to answer

00:24:46.470 --> 00:24:49.730
which of these three is
responsible for producing the data.

00:24:49.730 --> 00:24:52.220
But if you can do interventions,

00:24:52.220 --> 00:24:54.360
if you can go in there and
like poke inside the system,

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:57.040
and for instance, set the variable x

00:24:57.040 --> 00:24:59.310
to be a particular value,

00:24:59.310 --> 00:25:02.912
and that's the notation here is do(X).

00:25:02.912 --> 00:25:05.680
The do operator means
you're going and intervening

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:09.026
and if it was a simulator,
what you would do is you just,

00:25:09.026 --> 00:25:10.530
at every step of the program,

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:12.770
you just make sure x is equal to three.

00:25:12.770 --> 00:25:14.710
So you just see extra lines stuck in there

00:25:14.710 --> 00:25:16.570
where you force x to be three.

00:25:16.570 --> 00:25:19.490
And if you do that, then
if you look at the data

00:25:19.490 --> 00:25:21.130
that comes out of this thing,

00:25:21.130 --> 00:25:23.220
x will be three, no surprise there,

00:25:23.220 --> 00:25:25.390
but the distribution
of y will be different

00:25:25.390 --> 00:25:27.160
in these different cases.

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:28.840
And so that points to the fact

00:25:28.840 --> 00:25:30.570
that if you can do interventions,

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:33.570
you can answer a richer set
of questions than you can

00:25:33.570 --> 00:25:36.450
if you look at just data alone.

00:25:36.450 --> 00:25:38.200
Okay, so you need to be able

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:39.925
to make those kinds of interventions

00:25:39.925 --> 00:25:41.575
and I'll come back to that later.

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:45.380
Okay, so that is all a
little bit of a preamble

00:25:45.380 --> 00:25:47.100
and now I'm going to switch to a topic

00:25:47.100 --> 00:25:49.720
which is sort of near
and dear to my heart,

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:52.420
which I refer to as a
simulation-based inference.

00:25:52.420 --> 00:25:55.080
So I'm gonna try to sort
of set it up broadly,

00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:57.840
give a feel for why it's important,

00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:02.420
and tie it back to some
specific examples in physics.

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:05.800
And then later we'll go
back to kind of science

00:26:05.800 --> 00:26:10.650
and machine learning and
causation and things like that.

00:26:10.650 --> 00:26:15.550
Okay, so the first slide in this section

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:18.400
starts with the observation
that science is replete

00:26:18.400 --> 00:26:20.630
with high-fidelity simulations.

00:26:20.630 --> 00:26:23.790
Okay, so we have simulators
for our particle colliders

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:25.370
at the Large Hadron Collider.

00:26:25.370 --> 00:26:27.944
We have simulations for neuronal activity.

00:26:27.944 --> 00:26:32.260
We have simulations for
how epidemics spread.

00:26:32.260 --> 00:26:34.180
We have simulations for
gravitational lensing

00:26:34.180 --> 00:26:37.410
and the evolution of the
universe on the largest scales.

00:26:37.410 --> 00:26:40.670
We have simulators for how
air moves around an airplane,

00:26:40.670 --> 00:26:42.450
which we use to design airplanes,

00:26:42.450 --> 00:26:45.310
and simulations for how proteins will fold

00:26:45.310 --> 00:26:46.143
and things like that,

00:26:46.143 --> 00:26:49.410
which helps us into the
design of drugs and et cetera.

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:52.350
And these simulators, what's
interesting about them is

00:26:52.350 --> 00:26:55.563
not only can you use them
to generate synthetic data,

00:26:56.440 --> 00:27:01.440
they are causal models of
the data generating process.

00:27:01.870 --> 00:27:04.130
They actually have some mechanism of

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:05.820
what you think is happening, right?

00:27:05.820 --> 00:27:10.820
So in the case of proteins,
you have atoms and molecules,

00:27:10.820 --> 00:27:13.770
and they're maybe surrounded
by water, bumping into them,

00:27:13.770 --> 00:27:17.710
and you have energy for some arrangement

00:27:17.710 --> 00:27:20.450
and the system evolves in a certain way.

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:22.960
For epidemics, you have
individual people maybe

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:25.410
that you're modeling,
and they may get sick

00:27:25.410 --> 00:27:26.600
or not with some probability

00:27:26.600 --> 00:27:28.543
and that system evolves in some way.

00:27:30.013 --> 00:27:31.580
And so, you can imagine in that simulator,

00:27:31.580 --> 00:27:33.110
for instance, intervening,

00:27:33.110 --> 00:27:35.247
like, "What happened if I
like held onto the protein

00:27:35.247 --> 00:27:37.610
"and pulled it in a certain
way, what would happen?"

00:27:37.610 --> 00:27:39.650
Or in the epidemics, you
could do a simulation of

00:27:39.650 --> 00:27:42.550
what would happen if everyone
got a vaccine, for instance.

00:27:44.060 --> 00:27:47.030
So the fact that their
causal is very important.

00:27:47.030 --> 00:27:50.070
And now, as you'll see
throughout the talk,

00:27:50.070 --> 00:27:51.990
there's a kind of computational bent

00:27:51.990 --> 00:27:54.640
to a lot of the way that
I'm thinking about things.

00:27:54.640 --> 00:27:58.500
And so it's important to
recognize that the expressiveness

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:02.960
of programming languages
and the modern computing

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:05.750
and all of this allows us
to really scale this up

00:28:05.750 --> 00:28:10.310
and actually simulate these
very complicated systems

00:28:10.310 --> 00:28:12.997
and make some very
high-fidelity simulations.

00:28:12.997 --> 00:28:14.780
And that's something, that's a capability

00:28:14.780 --> 00:28:17.420
we didn't have 50 years ago.

00:28:17.420 --> 00:28:20.260
And so this is definitely changing the way

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:22.060
that we do science.

00:28:22.060 --> 00:28:23.490
But as you'll see in a second,

00:28:23.490 --> 00:28:24.730
it's a little bit out of step

00:28:24.730 --> 00:28:28.280
with the way that we approach
statistics currently.

00:28:28.280 --> 00:28:30.110
And so that's the point here, basically,

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:32.250
is that unfortunately, these simulators,

00:28:32.250 --> 00:28:35.360
while they produce very
high-fidelity simulated data,

00:28:35.360 --> 00:28:38.230
are poorly suited for
statistical inference.

00:28:38.230 --> 00:28:39.230
And so why is that?

00:28:39.230 --> 00:28:42.614
Well, let me try to give you
some feel for why that is.

00:28:42.614 --> 00:28:43.830
So before doing that,

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:46.980
I'm just gonna lay out a
little bit of notation.

00:28:46.980 --> 00:28:49.530
So I talked earlier about,
on the left side here,

00:28:49.530 --> 00:28:51.300
I have basically the theory.

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:54.032
It has some parameters
which I'm calling theta.

00:28:54.032 --> 00:28:56.750
And those might be, for instance,

00:28:56.750 --> 00:28:57.950
in the case of COVID or something,

00:28:57.950 --> 00:29:01.470
it might be like, how
virulent is this new virus?

00:29:01.470 --> 00:29:03.960
How quickly does it double

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:05.720
as it goes through the population?

00:29:05.720 --> 00:29:08.890
It could be a parameter like
the cosmological constant

00:29:08.890 --> 00:29:10.150
of the universe, or the mass

00:29:10.150 --> 00:29:12.210
of the Higgs boson, what have you.

00:29:12.210 --> 00:29:14.630
So you have these kinds
of parameters over here,

00:29:14.630 --> 00:29:16.770
and then the simulator allows you

00:29:16.770 --> 00:29:19.750
to make predictions for the observed data.

00:29:19.750 --> 00:29:20.970
It's not gonna just be,

00:29:20.970 --> 00:29:22.820
the observed data's not gonna
have a particular value.

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:25.100
It's gonna have many possible values.

00:29:25.100 --> 00:29:27.050
So it's gonna describe a
probability distribution

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:28.900
over the observed data.

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:30.940
But inside the simulation,

00:29:30.940 --> 00:29:32.900
there might also be other random variables

00:29:32.900 --> 00:29:35.550
that are referred to as latent variables,

00:29:35.550 --> 00:29:39.500
which are part of the story
of how the data came to be,

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:40.740
even though you might not be able

00:29:40.740 --> 00:29:42.510
to actually observe
them in your experiment.

00:29:42.510 --> 00:29:44.920
So I'll give some examples,

00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:46.500
but you could imagine that for instance,

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:47.920
in the case of COVID,

00:29:47.920 --> 00:29:50.280
x might just be like hospitalization rates

00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:53.666
or something like that, or people testing,

00:29:53.666 --> 00:29:56.380
how many people tested
positive with COVID?

00:29:56.380 --> 00:29:58.050
The latent variable
would be something like

00:29:58.050 --> 00:29:59.350
how many people actually have COVID?

00:29:59.350 --> 00:30:00.600
You don't know that.

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:02.130
But if you're doing a simulation,

00:30:02.130 --> 00:30:04.563
you would have access to that variable.

00:30:05.798 --> 00:30:09.360
So then, the question
of this inverse problem

00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:11.490
or of inference is to go from the data x

00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:13.963
to say the parameters of interest theta.

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:18.910
Now here's that same desk toy.

00:30:18.910 --> 00:30:21.970
Now I'm just going to, I
don't know if you can see it,

00:30:21.970 --> 00:30:23.070
here's the actual toy.

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:25.740
You can imagine, I want to introduce

00:30:25.740 --> 00:30:26.760
some notion of a parameter.

00:30:26.760 --> 00:30:29.340
So let's say I tilt the toy to the left,

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:30.890
or I tilt the toy to the right.

00:30:31.770 --> 00:30:33.260
If I tilt it one way,

00:30:33.260 --> 00:30:35.910
it's going to bias the distribution

00:30:35.910 --> 00:30:37.510
so that it doesn't look the same.

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:41.640
So imagine that I want to try
to infer this parameter theta

00:30:41.640 --> 00:30:45.780
which is say like how
tilted the whole toy is.

00:30:45.780 --> 00:30:47.590
I could relate that
maybe with a probability

00:30:47.590 --> 00:30:49.770
that a ball bounces to the right.

00:30:49.770 --> 00:30:51.920
And so that's the thing
that I want to infer

00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:54.480
and I'm gonna do that
based on the distribution

00:30:54.480 --> 00:30:57.000
of where the balls land at the bottom.

00:30:57.000 --> 00:30:59.230
And I'm gonna call the location

00:30:59.230 --> 00:31:02.000
where they land at the bottom x.

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:04.410
And then the latent
variable would be the path

00:31:04.410 --> 00:31:07.500
that the balls took through
this lattice of nails, okay?

00:31:07.500 --> 00:31:09.870
And, in principle, I could
maybe go observe that,

00:31:09.870 --> 00:31:12.320
but I wasn't paying any
attention, it was too fast.

00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:14.910
So really, all I get to
observe is where they landed

00:31:14.910 --> 00:31:16.500
and I'm gonna think of the path

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:18.690
that they took as the latent variable.

00:31:18.690 --> 00:31:21.500
So if you wanna predict
what is that distribution,

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:23.503
well, you might say,
okay, well, I can do that.

00:31:23.503 --> 00:31:25.190
It's either gonna bounce to the left

00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:26.972
or to the right with some probability.

00:31:26.972 --> 00:31:29.480
There's many possible paths,

00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:32.370
but there's some nice
symmetry that's there.

00:31:32.370 --> 00:31:35.160
and if you took your combinatorics class,

00:31:35.160 --> 00:31:36.310
then you can figure out what

00:31:36.310 --> 00:31:38.180
that distribution should look like.

00:31:38.180 --> 00:31:39.930
And there it is at the bottom,

00:31:39.930 --> 00:31:41.730
it's the binomial distribution.

00:31:41.730 --> 00:31:45.150
Okay, so you would assume this
is the binomial distribution

00:31:45.150 --> 00:31:48.440
as I did once before teaching a class

00:31:48.440 --> 00:31:50.440
on the subject to undergraduates.

00:31:50.440 --> 00:31:53.427
But then I compared that to
what it actually looks like

00:31:53.427 --> 00:31:55.000
and it doesn't match up.

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:56.790
And then I was a little confused as to why

00:31:56.790 --> 00:31:58.640
and then I took the slow-motion video

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:00.980
that I showed you before,
and if you look closely,

00:32:00.980 --> 00:32:04.290
you'll see that the balls are
not just going left and right.

00:32:04.290 --> 00:32:05.740
They're bouncing all over the place.

00:32:05.740 --> 00:32:08.670
Sometimes they skip
over two or three pins,

00:32:08.670 --> 00:32:10.610
they run into each other,

00:32:10.610 --> 00:32:12.850
it's a lot more complicated, right?

00:32:12.850 --> 00:32:13.750
So if you wanted to say

00:32:13.750 --> 00:32:16.460
what's the distribution
here down at the bottom,

00:32:16.460 --> 00:32:18.880
I have no idea what that distribution is,

00:32:18.880 --> 00:32:20.090
but I can simulate it.

00:32:20.090 --> 00:32:23.300
And that's kind of part of the point.

00:32:23.300 --> 00:32:25.670
So here's an example of a simulation

00:32:25.670 --> 00:32:29.550
for this exact thing done by
my colleague, (indistinct)

00:32:29.550 --> 00:32:31.670
and I know it looks very realistic.

00:32:31.670 --> 00:32:33.770
It looks a lot like that
video I just showed you.

00:32:33.770 --> 00:32:35.780
You don't know exactly
how bouncy the balls are,

00:32:35.780 --> 00:32:38.180
how big the nails, or
the pins are, et cetera,

00:32:38.180 --> 00:32:40.140
but you can simulate it.

00:32:40.140 --> 00:32:42.440
And symbolically, if you ask yourself,

00:32:42.440 --> 00:32:45.081
what's the probability distribution

00:32:45.081 --> 00:32:49.183
of the data landing in these
different bins at the bottom,

00:32:49.183 --> 00:32:52.490
given the parameter that
says like how tilted it is,

00:32:52.490 --> 00:32:54.870
symbolically, it's equal to,

00:32:54.870 --> 00:32:56.350
don't be worried about the math too much.

00:32:56.350 --> 00:32:57.570
This integral just means I'm going

00:32:57.570 --> 00:33:00.230
to sum over all the possible trajectories

00:33:00.230 --> 00:33:01.510
that the balls could take

00:33:01.510 --> 00:33:03.820
that would give you that same observation.

00:33:03.820 --> 00:33:06.680
The problem now is that
I can't do that sum

00:33:06.680 --> 00:33:10.040
with nice mathematical
tricks of combinatorics

00:33:11.034 --> 00:33:13.787
'cause there's no like
nice structure anymore

00:33:13.787 --> 00:33:15.870
to take advantage of.

00:33:15.870 --> 00:33:18.010
And this latent space of
different possibilities

00:33:18.010 --> 00:33:20.550
is growing very, very rapidly.

00:33:20.550 --> 00:33:23.250
And so that means that
basically this integral

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:24.410
that I want to perform

00:33:24.410 --> 00:33:27.518
where I sum over all the different
paths is now intractable.

00:33:27.518 --> 00:33:29.470
And that means that the likelihood,

00:33:29.470 --> 00:33:31.030
the thing that relates to the probability

00:33:31.030 --> 00:33:34.310
of the data given the
parameters is not available

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:36.720
to me anymore in any practical sense.

00:33:36.720 --> 00:33:40.010
And so here, I would say that
the likelihood is intractable

00:33:40.010 --> 00:33:41.360
and that means that basically all

00:33:41.360 --> 00:33:42.230
of the different approaches

00:33:42.230 --> 00:33:44.660
for doing statistical inference require

00:33:44.660 --> 00:33:46.060
that you have the likelihood,

00:33:46.060 --> 00:33:48.890
and so like, what do
you do now, basically?

00:33:48.890 --> 00:33:51.550
So while that, the
likelihood is intractable,

00:33:51.550 --> 00:33:54.320
I can still generate
synthetic observations

00:33:54.320 --> 00:33:56.190
with my simulator.

00:33:56.190 --> 00:33:57.450
And so it seems like you should be able

00:33:57.450 --> 00:33:58.740
to make some kind of progress

00:33:58.740 --> 00:34:00.760
by basically comparing the outcome

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:03.140
of the simulations to the actual data.

00:34:03.140 --> 00:34:04.110
And so then the question is just

00:34:04.110 --> 00:34:06.193
how are you going to do that in practice?

00:34:07.240 --> 00:34:09.320
So let me go back to the
Large Hadron Collider

00:34:09.320 --> 00:34:11.100
and tell a slight, the same story,

00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:16.090
but with a more complicated,
real-world science experiment.

00:34:16.090 --> 00:34:17.780
So here's the very complicated data

00:34:17.780 --> 00:34:19.500
at the Large Hadron Collider.

00:34:19.500 --> 00:34:21.053
It's very high dimensional.

00:34:22.760 --> 00:34:25.423
The detector has about
100,000 sensors on it.

00:34:27.730 --> 00:34:29.860
And so when I want to make a
prediction of what will happen,

00:34:29.860 --> 00:34:32.300
I start off with a theory, which is based

00:34:32.300 --> 00:34:34.950
on quantum mechanics
and quantum field theory

00:34:34.950 --> 00:34:36.100
that we call the standard model.

00:34:36.100 --> 00:34:37.373
It has some parameters.

00:34:38.440 --> 00:34:41.000
I can then use that theory, I don't know,

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:43.470
maybe you've seen things
like Feynman diagrams,

00:34:43.470 --> 00:34:45.050
sort of pictorial representations

00:34:45.050 --> 00:34:48.520
that allow me to calculate
what would happen,

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:50.190
what's the probability that the particles

00:34:50.190 --> 00:34:51.957
are gonna scatter off of
each other in a certain way

00:34:51.957 --> 00:34:54.270
and that this interaction will take place.

00:34:54.270 --> 00:34:56.368
This interaction has a Higgs boson in it

00:34:56.368 --> 00:34:58.670
that decays into some
other particles called Z's

00:34:58.670 --> 00:35:00.870
that decay into other things.

00:35:00.870 --> 00:35:02.030
Doesn't really matter.

00:35:02.030 --> 00:35:04.900
But these particles over
here on the right are coming,

00:35:04.900 --> 00:35:06.640
flying out into the detector.

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:09.223
The problem is that we
don't see those directly.

00:35:10.130 --> 00:35:11.610
Instead, there's another process

00:35:11.610 --> 00:35:13.973
where they radiate and
it becomes really messy.

00:35:15.913 --> 00:35:17.230
And at this stage,

00:35:17.230 --> 00:35:19.080
I can't write down what's going on

00:35:19.080 --> 00:35:20.850
like really on a pencil and paper.

00:35:20.850 --> 00:35:22.810
I can describe what happens kinda locally.

00:35:22.810 --> 00:35:24.450
Every time you see one
of these splittings,

00:35:24.450 --> 00:35:26.315
I can write down a probability.

00:35:26.315 --> 00:35:29.223
But the whole process is
this very complicated thing.

00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:33.410
And so I can put it on a computer,

00:35:33.410 --> 00:35:35.090
but it's very difficult to work with

00:35:35.090 --> 00:35:36.520
with a pencil and paper.

00:35:36.520 --> 00:35:41.520
So you go from the latent
variables that were associated

00:35:41.700 --> 00:35:44.890
with this first stage,
followed by latent variables,

00:35:44.890 --> 00:35:47.280
random variables that
I don't get to observe,

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:50.010
followed with this
complicated showering process,

00:35:50.010 --> 00:35:51.230
is what it's called.

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:53.530
Then these particles
are physical particles

00:35:53.530 --> 00:35:56.420
that come flying and
smash into our detectors

00:35:56.420 --> 00:36:00.310
and they ionize and they
run into pieces of metal

00:36:00.310 --> 00:36:01.323
and they do all the things that they do.

00:36:02.270 --> 00:36:05.430
And then there are literally hundreds

00:36:05.430 --> 00:36:07.450
of millions of latent variables associated

00:36:07.450 --> 00:36:09.840
with how these particles interact

00:36:09.840 --> 00:36:13.050
with our gargantuan detectors.

00:36:13.050 --> 00:36:17.990
And finally you get observed
you electronic signals

00:36:17.990 --> 00:36:20.560
in our detector, which
is like the raw data

00:36:20.560 --> 00:36:23.200
that is collected by these experiments,

00:36:23.200 --> 00:36:24.610
and so that's x.

00:36:24.610 --> 00:36:27.874
And so if I wanna think
about how do I simulate that,

00:36:27.874 --> 00:36:29.560
I just told you the physical story,

00:36:29.560 --> 00:36:32.150
there's actually software
for each one of these stages.

00:36:32.150 --> 00:36:33.720
And you can think of
these different programs

00:36:33.720 --> 00:36:36.416
as basically encoding these
probability distributions

00:36:36.416 --> 00:36:38.570
that go from one step to the
next step, to the next step.

00:36:38.570 --> 00:36:40.850
And it's really kind of hopping

00:36:40.850 --> 00:36:43.170
from the smallest scales
to the next scale,

00:36:43.170 --> 00:36:46.020
to the next scale, in
some probabilistic way,

00:36:46.020 --> 00:36:48.090
until you get to the final observations.

00:36:48.090 --> 00:36:50.900
And we call these Monte Carlo generators

00:36:50.900 --> 00:36:53.240
and that's because Monte
Carlo is a technique

00:36:53.240 --> 00:36:55.410
that you can see as a way of trying

00:36:55.410 --> 00:36:58.120
to integrate these random variables.

00:36:58.120 --> 00:37:00.083
So symbolically, you could write down

00:37:00.083 --> 00:37:02.650
this simulation this way.

00:37:02.650 --> 00:37:03.730
You're saying basically the likelihood

00:37:03.730 --> 00:37:06.890
of the observations
given the data is equal

00:37:06.890 --> 00:37:11.170
to this integral of this
product of probabilities.

00:37:11.170 --> 00:37:13.920
But this integral is like
100-million-dimensional

00:37:13.920 --> 00:37:15.110
and there's no way that you're going

00:37:15.110 --> 00:37:16.140
to do that, basically.

00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:20.601
Like, this is a totally
intractable integral in practice.

00:37:20.601 --> 00:37:23.870
So that means that the
likelihood is intractable here.

00:37:23.870 --> 00:37:25.220
So what do you do?

00:37:25.220 --> 00:37:28.010
Like, how is it that we
discovered the Higgs boson

00:37:28.010 --> 00:37:30.290
if we're in this kind of setting, right?

00:37:30.290 --> 00:37:34.010
So what we have done
as a field for decades

00:37:34.010 --> 00:37:36.770
and other fields have kind of
developed their own version

00:37:36.770 --> 00:37:40.500
of this ranging from
genomics to cosmology,

00:37:40.500 --> 00:37:44.160
to all over the place,
is that you use your kind

00:37:44.160 --> 00:37:46.020
of domain knowledge,
your expert knowledge,

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:48.130
to think about like, well,
what is it about the data

00:37:48.130 --> 00:37:51.060
that's going to be useful
for testing the hypothesis

00:37:51.060 --> 00:37:52.810
that I have in mind?

00:37:52.810 --> 00:37:55.600
And that turns into
basically in the context

00:37:55.600 --> 00:37:58.820
of the particle physics, taking
very high-dimensional data

00:37:58.820 --> 00:38:02.870
from the detector and then
distilling 100 million

00:38:02.870 --> 00:38:05.451
electronic sensor readouts
into just one number,

00:38:05.451 --> 00:38:08.220
which is the horizontal axis in this plot,

00:38:08.220 --> 00:38:10.633
which I'm going to then
make a histogram of.

00:38:11.615 --> 00:38:13.100
And I'm gonna histogram the data

00:38:13.100 --> 00:38:15.000
so every collision I have just one number

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:17.740
and I have many collisions,
so I build a histogram,

00:38:17.740 --> 00:38:20.070
and that's like the black
dots in the histogram.

00:38:20.070 --> 00:38:21.740
And the red histogram is basically

00:38:21.740 --> 00:38:24.290
what I predict from my theory.

00:38:24.290 --> 00:38:27.280
And the important part is
that I can sort of fill

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:30.420
that histogram up with
a bunch of simulations.

00:38:30.420 --> 00:38:32.990
So if I step back and
like look at the text

00:38:32.990 --> 00:38:35.600
on the slide here, and statistically,

00:38:35.600 --> 00:38:37.600
you would say this one variable

00:38:37.600 --> 00:38:39.160
that distills all the information

00:38:39.160 --> 00:38:41.300
from this high-dimensional data

00:38:41.300 --> 00:38:42.820
is called a summary statistic.

00:38:42.820 --> 00:38:45.100
That's what a statistician would call it,

00:38:45.100 --> 00:38:47.320
sometimes related to feature engineering

00:38:47.320 --> 00:38:49.413
is what a machine learning
person might call it.

00:38:50.710 --> 00:38:54.720
Coming up with this summary
quantity is basically the task

00:38:54.720 --> 00:38:56.320
of a skilled physicist.

00:38:56.320 --> 00:38:58.460
And then we try to
estimate the distribution

00:38:58.460 --> 00:39:01.150
of the summary statistic, here
are these like histograms,

00:39:01.150 --> 00:39:04.080
using just something
simple like a histogram.

00:39:04.080 --> 00:39:06.640
So if we look at this
picture of this collision

00:39:06.640 --> 00:39:10.020
that I showed you earlier
which was a candidate

00:39:10.020 --> 00:39:11.642
for the Higgs boson,

00:39:11.642 --> 00:39:14.010
we have this very high-dimensional data,

00:39:14.010 --> 00:39:15.380
what does the process look like

00:39:15.380 --> 00:39:16.740
that we take this high-dimensional data

00:39:16.740 --> 00:39:19.230
and we distill it into a single number?

00:39:19.230 --> 00:39:21.580
Well, the first part we
kind of take for granted,

00:39:21.580 --> 00:39:23.590
like you can see in the picture,

00:39:23.590 --> 00:39:26.890
there are these, for instance,
these four red lines.

00:39:26.890 --> 00:39:28.337
If you ask a particle
physicist, they'll say,

00:39:28.337 --> 00:39:29.680
"Oh, these are four muons

00:39:30.557 --> 00:39:33.437
"that were reconstructed
from the particular signals

00:39:33.437 --> 00:39:35.890
"that they leave inside of the detector,"

00:39:35.890 --> 00:39:38.390
because muons have this
interesting property

00:39:38.390 --> 00:39:40.600
that they can punch their way all the way

00:39:40.600 --> 00:39:42.800
through all of this material,

00:39:42.800 --> 00:39:47.089
there's like several meters of
lead and iron and et cetera.

00:39:47.089 --> 00:39:48.680
So they can go all the way through that,

00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:50.707
but they ionize on their way out

00:39:50.707 --> 00:39:52.260
and that leaves a little trace

00:39:52.260 --> 00:39:54.650
of energy deposits along the way.

00:39:54.650 --> 00:39:56.970
And so we can see those energy deposits

00:39:56.970 --> 00:39:58.777
and then we can identify
them and then say,

00:39:58.777 --> 00:40:01.040
"Yes, we think there was a muon here."

00:40:01.040 --> 00:40:04.200
We can say if it was a positive
or negatively charged one.

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:06.620
We can estimate its
energy and its direction

00:40:06.620 --> 00:40:07.790
and then that's basically all there is

00:40:07.790 --> 00:40:08.963
to say about that muon.

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:12.780
And so then in this example,
we have four of them.

00:40:12.780 --> 00:40:14.870
So we don't, there's already lots

00:40:14.870 --> 00:40:17.510
of philosophical
questions here about like,

00:40:17.510 --> 00:40:18.860
how do you know that that's a muon?

00:40:18.860 --> 00:40:20.050
You don't really see the muon.

00:40:20.050 --> 00:40:22.960
You see the energy
deposits in the detector.

00:40:22.960 --> 00:40:26.810
But this concept of a muon has
served physicists very well.

00:40:26.810 --> 00:40:29.087
We see muons in all sorts
of different experiments

00:40:29.087 --> 00:40:31.060
and we can predict what
they're gonna look like

00:40:31.060 --> 00:40:32.700
and that's exactly what
we see in the data.

00:40:32.700 --> 00:40:34.840
So physicists generally
believe that the idea

00:40:34.840 --> 00:40:36.280
of muons are real, right?

00:40:36.280 --> 00:40:39.930
So if I take these four
muons, that's great.

00:40:39.930 --> 00:40:42.540
Maybe I can sort of see
them in my detector.

00:40:42.540 --> 00:40:44.240
But what about the Higgs?

00:40:44.240 --> 00:40:47.450
Well, if I produced the
Higgs in this collision,

00:40:47.450 --> 00:40:49.760
it would've decayed instantaneously

00:40:49.760 --> 00:40:53.170
and never even made it
to the next atom over

00:40:53.170 --> 00:40:54.203
before it decayed.

00:40:55.067 --> 00:40:57.190
So we're never going
to see the Higgs boson

00:40:57.190 --> 00:40:59.170
in our detector directly.

00:40:59.170 --> 00:41:02.200
So how do we know if we made a Higgs?

00:41:02.200 --> 00:41:04.740
Well, we want to try to somehow

00:41:04.740 --> 00:41:08.010
basically sequentially
invert the causal process

00:41:08.010 --> 00:41:09.750
of how the data came to be.

00:41:09.750 --> 00:41:12.590
So first we identified these muons

00:41:12.590 --> 00:41:13.750
and now we wanna think about,

00:41:13.750 --> 00:41:15.800
okay, well, where do the muons come from?

00:41:16.790 --> 00:41:18.223
And then we're going to exploit things

00:41:18.223 --> 00:41:20.513
that we know, like relativity.

00:41:21.500 --> 00:41:23.889
So maybe you're familiar
with E equals MC squared.

00:41:23.889 --> 00:41:26.260
That's not really what Einstein said.

00:41:26.260 --> 00:41:28.940
That only kind of works
if nothing's moving.

00:41:28.940 --> 00:41:30.510
If something's moving,

00:41:30.510 --> 00:41:32.350
then you can sort of square both sides

00:41:32.350 --> 00:41:35.600
and add an extra component that
has to do with the momentum.

00:41:35.600 --> 00:41:37.450
So you'd say the energy
is related to the mass

00:41:37.450 --> 00:41:39.400
and the momentum through this equation.

00:41:40.392 --> 00:41:42.380
But I don't see the Higgs particle.

00:41:42.380 --> 00:41:45.220
So how do I use this equation?

00:41:45.220 --> 00:41:46.607
Well, you can take advantage of energy

00:41:46.607 --> 00:41:48.230
and momentum conservation.

00:41:48.230 --> 00:41:50.290
So if there was a Higgs that was produced,

00:41:50.290 --> 00:41:51.860
the energy that it had was kind of

00:41:51.860 --> 00:41:55.350
like the energy beforehand,
turns into the energy after,

00:41:55.350 --> 00:41:58.730
which is the energy that I
would have in those four muons.

00:41:58.730 --> 00:42:01.233
And similarly, the momentum for the Higgs

00:42:01.233 --> 00:42:03.030
is the momentum before,

00:42:03.030 --> 00:42:04.800
turns into the momentum after,

00:42:04.800 --> 00:42:07.930
turns into the sum of the
momentum and those four muons.

00:42:07.930 --> 00:42:10.147
So I can take this
equation and this equation

00:42:10.147 --> 00:42:12.280
and put them together and rearrange it.

00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:14.317
And basically if I'm
producing Higgs bosons

00:42:14.317 --> 00:42:17.010
and the Higgs bosons
have a well-defined mass,

00:42:17.010 --> 00:42:19.320
then this particular combination

00:42:19.320 --> 00:42:22.740
of things should basically
always give me the same number up

00:42:22.740 --> 00:42:24.513
to like measurement uncertainties.

00:42:26.017 --> 00:42:28.380
So let's see what it
looks like in the data.

00:42:28.380 --> 00:42:30.430
That's this quantity that I just told you.

00:42:30.430 --> 00:42:32.500
For every collision, I
calculate that equation

00:42:32.500 --> 00:42:35.230
I just showed you and I
get a particular number

00:42:35.230 --> 00:42:36.240
and I histogram it.

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:39.200
And then I can do that
with both real data.

00:42:39.200 --> 00:42:40.540
Those are the black dots,

00:42:40.540 --> 00:42:42.430
and then I can do it with simulated data.

00:42:42.430 --> 00:42:43.930
So if I take the simulation of

00:42:43.930 --> 00:42:47.490
what I expect from normal processes

00:42:47.490 --> 00:42:49.950
that I already know about
in particle physics,

00:42:49.950 --> 00:42:51.750
I expect the blue histogram

00:42:51.750 --> 00:42:54.250
that's sort of sitting on
top of the screen histogram,

00:42:54.250 --> 00:42:57.390
and that's basically plays the
role of our null hypothesis.

00:42:57.390 --> 00:43:00.480
That's what we expect to see
if there is no Higgs particle.

00:43:00.480 --> 00:43:02.300
And these are the little Feynman diagrams

00:43:02.300 --> 00:43:04.960
that correspond to those processes.

00:43:04.960 --> 00:43:06.060
But if I'm producing the Higgs,

00:43:06.060 --> 00:43:08.207
I expect to see something
else, I have a new process,

00:43:08.207 --> 00:43:11.210
and so I expect to see a
bump sitting on top of it.

00:43:11.210 --> 00:43:12.630
And so that's the prediction

00:43:12.630 --> 00:43:14.660
and you see there's this very
sharp feature in the data,

00:43:14.660 --> 00:43:16.220
this very sharp bump.

00:43:16.220 --> 00:43:19.600
And the fact that it's a
sharp feature is important.

00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:21.480
And the reason that
the way that I was able

00:43:21.480 --> 00:43:24.700
to use my domain knowledge
to take the very complicated

00:43:24.700 --> 00:43:28.820
from the LHC data and come up
with this very sharp feature

00:43:28.820 --> 00:43:33.811
is like a testament to what
physicists are good at, right?

00:43:33.811 --> 00:43:35.840
And so as you can already see,

00:43:35.840 --> 00:43:38.000
the data look like they prefer a bump,

00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:39.250
and as we collected more and more data,

00:43:39.250 --> 00:43:41.900
the evidence for the
bump became very obvious.

00:43:41.900 --> 00:43:45.210
And that is the, basically
the experimental signature

00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:46.410
that we found the Higgs.

00:43:47.330 --> 00:43:48.420
But we didn't just do that

00:43:48.420 --> 00:43:50.350
in the one example that I showed you.

00:43:50.350 --> 00:43:53.280
This one, we did it
across literally hundreds

00:43:53.280 --> 00:43:54.850
of different ways that you might expect

00:43:54.850 --> 00:43:58.450
to see the Higgs particle in the LHC data,

00:43:58.450 --> 00:44:00.110
and they're all related to each other.

00:44:00.110 --> 00:44:01.650
And this is where this collaborative,

00:44:01.650 --> 00:44:03.180
statistical modeling framework

00:44:03.180 --> 00:44:08.040
that Priya alluded to came into play.

00:44:08.040 --> 00:44:10.100
And this is just a graph of the like,

00:44:10.100 --> 00:44:13.180
the computer, like the computer program

00:44:13.180 --> 00:44:15.830
that we used to calculate the probability

00:44:15.830 --> 00:44:18.800
of the data, given the parameters.

00:44:18.800 --> 00:44:20.420
It's pretty complicated,

00:44:20.420 --> 00:44:23.600
but it's really like classical statistics.

00:44:23.600 --> 00:44:25.230
And the fact that we're able to relate all

00:44:25.230 --> 00:44:28.020
of these different kinds of
parts of the data is, again,

00:44:28.020 --> 00:44:29.620
like shows the strength of the fact

00:44:29.620 --> 00:44:31.640
that our theory has this causal nature

00:44:31.640 --> 00:44:33.310
that allows us to relate all of these

00:44:33.310 --> 00:44:36.020
to different parts of the data together.

00:44:36.020 --> 00:44:39.133
So then we analyzed
this and unsurprisingly,

00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:42.463
we found the Higgs and
we claimed the discovery.

00:44:43.390 --> 00:44:46.380
So I showed you this picture
before the slide previously

00:44:46.380 --> 00:44:48.280
so that we took the high dimensional data

00:44:48.280 --> 00:44:51.890
and we boiled it down
into the summary statistic

00:44:51.890 --> 00:44:53.120
and this has worked pretty well.

00:44:53.120 --> 00:44:56.450
So this is an example of
simulation-based inference

00:44:56.450 --> 00:44:57.470
where this histogram,

00:44:57.470 --> 00:45:00.690
the red histogram is
basically playing the role.

00:45:00.690 --> 00:45:02.090
It's approximating the probability

00:45:02.090 --> 00:45:03.690
of the data, given the theory

00:45:03.690 --> 00:45:06.440
and I can calculate that from simulations.

00:45:06.440 --> 00:45:08.190
The problem with this approach though,

00:45:08.190 --> 00:45:09.740
is it does not work well

00:45:09.740 --> 00:45:12.510
if you don't have a really
good summary statistic.

00:45:12.510 --> 00:45:13.840
And if you want to, for instance,

00:45:13.840 --> 00:45:15.810
work with like more like the raw data

00:45:15.810 --> 00:45:18.720
or your summary is like a
higher-dimensional thing,

00:45:18.720 --> 00:45:20.890
then this approach is not gonna scale.

00:45:20.890 --> 00:45:22.710
Now, basically, you're never gonna have

00:45:22.710 --> 00:45:26.170
enough simulated data to make
a high-dimensional histogram.

00:45:26.170 --> 00:45:27.330
And that's related to this thing

00:45:27.330 --> 00:45:29.080
called the curse of dimensionality.

00:45:31.201 --> 00:45:33.600
So the that's a bit of a problem.

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:36.600
And so the interesting thing
that's happening right now,

00:45:36.600 --> 00:45:38.820
which is using machine
learning, in some sense,

00:45:38.820 --> 00:45:40.800
in a very traditional way,

00:45:40.800 --> 00:45:43.620
is since you can use your simulator

00:45:43.620 --> 00:45:45.940
to produce a lot of synthetic data,

00:45:45.940 --> 00:45:48.550
it's a very good match
for machine learning.

00:45:48.550 --> 00:45:50.810
And what people have
figured out how to do,

00:45:50.810 --> 00:45:53.960
and I've made some
contributions here myself,

00:45:53.960 --> 00:45:57.260
is figure out how can we
use the simulated data

00:45:57.260 --> 00:45:59.880
and then the value of the
parameters that we know,

00:45:59.880 --> 00:46:02.230
'cause we use them when
we ran the simulator,

00:46:02.230 --> 00:46:04.870
to try to get a neural
network or something else,

00:46:04.870 --> 00:46:06.600
some other kind of machine-learning model

00:46:06.600 --> 00:46:09.485
to effectively approximate the likelihood

00:46:09.485 --> 00:46:11.477
or the likelihood ratio, or the posterior,

00:46:11.477 --> 00:46:13.260
one of these statistics objects

00:46:13.260 --> 00:46:15.060
that we need for doing inference?

00:46:15.060 --> 00:46:17.810
And basically it's just
a way of getting around

00:46:17.810 --> 00:46:20.740
this intractable integral
that I referred to earlier.

00:46:20.740 --> 00:46:23.140
And so in that sense, it's very practical,

00:46:23.140 --> 00:46:24.580
but it's also very profound

00:46:24.580 --> 00:46:26.240
because it's giving us like really,

00:46:26.240 --> 00:46:29.440
like superpowers across all
sorts of different areas

00:46:29.440 --> 00:46:32.340
of science and it's
happening like right now.

00:46:32.340 --> 00:46:35.750
And I think it's like a very
important, profound moment

00:46:35.750 --> 00:46:38.420
in the evolution of statistics.

00:46:38.420 --> 00:46:40.120
Previously, there were really big jumps

00:46:40.120 --> 00:46:42.460
that happened in statistics when you went

00:46:42.460 --> 00:46:45.310
from having to solve problems

00:46:45.310 --> 00:46:47.610
with just pencil and
paper and using calculus,

00:46:50.025 --> 00:46:51.970
to where you could, for instance,

00:46:51.970 --> 00:46:53.780
if you didn't know how to find the minimum

00:46:53.780 --> 00:46:54.920
or maximum of a function,

00:46:54.920 --> 00:46:57.640
you could use a numerical optimizer.

00:46:57.640 --> 00:47:00.130
Or if you didn't know how
to integrate something

00:47:00.130 --> 00:47:01.460
with a pencil and paper,

00:47:01.460 --> 00:47:04.930
you could use numerical
integration techniques.

00:47:04.930 --> 00:47:07.410
Now we're at the point where
the likelihood function itself

00:47:07.410 --> 00:47:10.440
is not tractable and we're
able to use machine learning

00:47:10.440 --> 00:47:12.720
to basically fill in that gap

00:47:12.720 --> 00:47:15.800
and give us a good
approximation of the likelihood,

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:17.940
which then allows us to go back

00:47:17.940 --> 00:47:21.040
to do kind of classical
statistical inference.

00:47:21.040 --> 00:47:23.960
So this is a really important point.

00:47:23.960 --> 00:47:28.530
And it's allowing scientists
of various stripes

00:47:28.530 --> 00:47:30.960
to be able to work kind of directly

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:33.120
with the low-level raw data.

00:47:33.120 --> 00:47:35.300
Maybe not have some
good insight about like,

00:47:35.300 --> 00:47:37.120
what is a good summary?

00:47:37.120 --> 00:47:38.970
But you're not throwing
away your expert knowledge.

00:47:38.970 --> 00:47:40.745
Your expert knowledge is in the simulator

00:47:40.745 --> 00:47:42.870
and you're using machine learning as a way

00:47:42.870 --> 00:47:45.659
of basically transferring
the expert knowledge

00:47:45.659 --> 00:47:48.110
from the simulator onto the surrogate,

00:47:48.110 --> 00:47:50.370
which is some sort of
neural network or something.

00:47:50.370 --> 00:47:53.550
And so, it's, again, I say
it's all very practical,

00:47:53.550 --> 00:47:56.143
but it has profound implications.

00:47:57.390 --> 00:47:59.870
Okay, so, yeah, so if I
step back a little bit

00:47:59.870 --> 00:48:03.020
and say, think about what's going on,

00:48:03.020 --> 00:48:04.750
you know, as I mentioned before,

00:48:04.750 --> 00:48:05.870
there are all these areas of science

00:48:05.870 --> 00:48:08.590
that have these well-motivated
mechanistic models

00:48:08.590 --> 00:48:10.640
that are the simulators,

00:48:10.640 --> 00:48:12.470
machine learning is basically providing

00:48:12.470 --> 00:48:14.143
an ability to effectively gap,

00:48:15.430 --> 00:48:20.000
bridge the gap between some
low-level microscopic phenomena

00:48:20.000 --> 00:48:23.210
that interact in some
way that's intractable,

00:48:23.210 --> 00:48:25.050
to some effective statistical model

00:48:25.050 --> 00:48:27.130
for some higher-level quantities,

00:48:27.130 --> 00:48:28.980
some other higher-level phenomena.

00:48:28.980 --> 00:48:32.590
And so it's kind of like the
more like emergent phenomena

00:48:32.590 --> 00:48:35.310
and you're able to bridge those
two in some effective way.

00:48:35.310 --> 00:48:37.220
And so I think that if you want

00:48:37.220 --> 00:48:39.410
to be able to do this reliably,
there's a question of like

00:48:39.410 --> 00:48:41.720
how much simulated data do you need?

00:48:41.720 --> 00:48:43.360
And this is where sometimes,

00:48:43.360 --> 00:48:45.490
if you don't have a smart way of doing it,

00:48:45.490 --> 00:48:47.090
this is where it's going to require

00:48:47.090 --> 00:48:48.770
an enormous amount of simulated data

00:48:48.770 --> 00:48:52.130
and this whole approach
is doomed to failure.

00:48:52.130 --> 00:48:55.820
But if there's something
that makes this work,

00:48:55.820 --> 00:48:56.993
then that's important.

00:48:59.350 --> 00:49:02.060
Part of the secret sauce here
is essentially causality.

00:49:02.060 --> 00:49:03.890
And this is where I think it connects

00:49:03.890 --> 00:49:05.430
to what James Woodward referred to

00:49:05.430 --> 00:49:07.480
as the more normative aspects

00:49:07.480 --> 00:49:12.390
about relating causality
and causal inference.

00:49:12.390 --> 00:49:14.080
Okay, so I'm looking at the time here.

00:49:14.080 --> 00:49:17.470
I realize I'm going slower
than I had intended,

00:49:17.470 --> 00:49:19.393
hopefully for the benefit of clarity.

00:49:21.052 --> 00:49:23.739
But okay, so I'm gonna switch a little bit

00:49:23.739 --> 00:49:26.730
and don't be alarmed if I kind
of skip over a few slides.

00:49:26.730 --> 00:49:28.613
So, okay.

00:49:30.002 --> 00:49:33.070
So what I just talked
about was sort of one part

00:49:33.070 --> 00:49:34.950
of kind of the bigger scientific method.

00:49:34.950 --> 00:49:38.210
This is one of these kind of toy drawings

00:49:38.210 --> 00:49:40.280
of the scientific method, which is,

00:49:40.280 --> 00:49:41.850
I don't really believe
the scientific method

00:49:41.850 --> 00:49:43.400
has a flowchart associated to it.

00:49:43.400 --> 00:49:46.167
But if there is one, I
prefer this one compared

00:49:46.167 --> 00:49:49.220
to the other ones that I
find with a Google search.

00:49:49.220 --> 00:49:51.910
But the thing that I
wanted to focus on here is

00:49:51.910 --> 00:49:55.833
that this part about gathering
data and testing predictions,

00:49:56.870 --> 00:49:59.790
that's essentially the part
that I was just describing.

00:49:59.790 --> 00:50:04.010
We're using machine learning
to enable statistical inference

00:50:04.010 --> 00:50:06.990
essentially for doing
hypothesis testing, okay.

00:50:06.990 --> 00:50:09.540
And so that, so you can either
see it testing predictions

00:50:09.540 --> 00:50:12.330
or maybe it's about
rejecting a hypothesis.

00:50:12.330 --> 00:50:15.760
So it's kind of isolated to
this part of this flowchart.

00:50:15.760 --> 00:50:19.920
Nowhere in there is there a
part that's about thinking

00:50:19.920 --> 00:50:23.430
of interesting questions,
formulating hypotheses,

00:50:23.430 --> 00:50:25.585
developing testable predictions,

00:50:25.585 --> 00:50:28.380
trying to figure out what
experiment should you do?

00:50:28.380 --> 00:50:30.290
All of those parts of
the scientific method

00:50:30.290 --> 00:50:33.860
are not reflected in what
I just described at all.

00:50:33.860 --> 00:50:36.056
So if you want to try to incorporate them,

00:50:36.056 --> 00:50:39.000
what is that gonna look like, right?

00:50:39.000 --> 00:50:42.370
So here is where you kind of
start to connect to other ideas

00:50:42.370 --> 00:50:43.657
and machine learning that
are more closely related

00:50:43.657 --> 00:50:46.290
to what's called like
reinforcement learning.

00:50:46.290 --> 00:50:49.500
So maybe you're aware of
the news that happened.

00:50:49.500 --> 00:50:52.210
Surprisingly, this was
six years ago today.

00:50:52.210 --> 00:50:56.340
March 9th, 2016 is when one of these,

00:50:56.340 --> 00:50:58.700
AlphaGo, one of these
reinforcement learning,

00:50:58.700 --> 00:51:01.500
deep neural network
type approaches was able

00:51:01.500 --> 00:51:06.010
to beat Lee Sedol in a game of Go.

00:51:06.010 --> 00:51:07.630
And if you think about what's happening,

00:51:07.630 --> 00:51:10.100
there's an iterative type of interaction

00:51:10.100 --> 00:51:13.690
between sort of the AI
agent in some environment,

00:51:13.690 --> 00:51:16.140
which is like the
opponent on the Go board,

00:51:16.140 --> 00:51:20.440
and the AI agent is trying to
decide what move to make next.

00:51:20.440 --> 00:51:22.330
And this looks a little bit like,

00:51:22.330 --> 00:51:24.360
here in reinforcement
learning, you have an agent,

00:51:24.360 --> 00:51:27.810
it takes some action,
interacts with the environment,

00:51:27.810 --> 00:51:30.310
and then there's some
sort of updated state,

00:51:30.310 --> 00:51:32.350
and maybe you get, the
agent gets a reward,

00:51:32.350 --> 00:51:33.590
which would be something like are they

00:51:33.590 --> 00:51:37.110
in a better position on the board or not?

00:51:37.110 --> 00:51:38.903
But this looks a lot like,
for instance, a scientist

00:51:38.903 --> 00:51:41.650
in that your scientist is the agent trying

00:51:41.650 --> 00:51:44.330
to decide, What experiment
should I perform next?"

00:51:44.330 --> 00:51:45.820
And then when they actually decide that,

00:51:45.820 --> 00:51:47.470
they interact with the
environment, which is actually

00:51:47.470 --> 00:51:50.220
like performing the
experiment, gathering data.

00:51:50.220 --> 00:51:53.370
Then the interpreter is basically
the statistical analysis.

00:51:53.370 --> 00:51:55.900
And the updated state is
essentially, for instance,

00:51:55.900 --> 00:51:58.010
like the Bayesian update
from your prior knowledge

00:51:58.010 --> 00:51:59.953
to your posterior belief, right?

00:51:59.953 --> 00:52:03.670
And so this is kind of a
nice, like schematic of

00:52:04.620 --> 00:52:07.190
what experimentation looks like

00:52:07.190 --> 00:52:09.350
that connects with machine learning.

00:52:09.350 --> 00:52:11.060
And this can all be formalized.

00:52:11.060 --> 00:52:12.837
I'm not gonna go through this,

00:52:12.837 --> 00:52:14.760
but this is the formalizing
it in the language

00:52:14.760 --> 00:52:16.540
of statistical decision theory,

00:52:16.540 --> 00:52:19.450
you see things like states of
nature, types of observations,

00:52:19.450 --> 00:52:21.140
what kind of actions you want to take.

00:52:21.140 --> 00:52:24.737
And again, you see
these normative concepts

00:52:24.737 --> 00:52:26.610
like loss functions and risk

00:52:26.610 --> 00:52:28.340
and all of these kinds of things.

00:52:28.340 --> 00:52:31.210
And then you can basically evaluate

00:52:31.210 --> 00:52:33.660
how well a particular
strategy is working or not.

00:52:35.572 --> 00:52:37.800
So if you wanted to
design a new experiment,

00:52:37.800 --> 00:52:40.560
you might say that, like the
thing that I'm going to use

00:52:40.560 --> 00:52:42.730
to decide which experiment
to perform next,

00:52:42.730 --> 00:52:45.300
or which intervention I
want to perform is the one

00:52:45.300 --> 00:52:48.490
that is going, an expectation is going

00:52:48.490 --> 00:52:52.050
to move me from my prior degree of belief

00:52:52.050 --> 00:52:53.640
to a posterior degree of belief

00:52:53.640 --> 00:52:57.250
where the information gain is maximized.

00:52:57.250 --> 00:52:58.477
And you can formalize all of that

00:52:58.477 --> 00:53:01.530
and in these equations, not so important.

00:53:01.530 --> 00:53:04.480
Any, oh, but I should say
what is important is the way

00:53:04.480 --> 00:53:07.140
that the fact that you're
able to answer this question,

00:53:07.140 --> 00:53:08.950
like, what do I expect would happen

00:53:08.950 --> 00:53:10.760
if I do a certain intervention?

00:53:10.760 --> 00:53:12.690
The only way you can answer
a question like that is

00:53:12.690 --> 00:53:14.350
if you have a causal model.

00:53:14.350 --> 00:53:16.470
And so, because the
simulator is a causal model,

00:53:16.470 --> 00:53:18.810
I can actually answer this question.

00:53:18.810 --> 00:53:21.320
And then if you look
the different quantities

00:53:21.320 --> 00:53:23.918
that pop up in here,
priors and posteriors,

00:53:23.918 --> 00:53:25.160
if you have a simulator,

00:53:25.160 --> 00:53:26.550
then you get back into the situation

00:53:26.550 --> 00:53:28.310
where the likelihood is intractable,

00:53:28.310 --> 00:53:29.200
but you can take advantage

00:53:29.200 --> 00:53:30.980
of these simulation-based inference things

00:53:30.980 --> 00:53:34.173
to try to incorporate the two
and do experimental design.

00:53:35.160 --> 00:53:36.480
So here's a bit of a flowchart.

00:53:36.480 --> 00:53:38.170
If I wanted to do experimental design

00:53:38.170 --> 00:53:40.090
where I'm sort of trying to optimize

00:53:40.090 --> 00:53:42.708
the expected information
gain of my next experiment,

00:53:42.708 --> 00:53:46.400
I sort of do an experiment,
I get some data.

00:53:46.400 --> 00:53:50.000
I use Bayes' theorem to update
my prior to a posterior,

00:53:50.000 --> 00:53:51.880
and then I wanna optimize what's my next

00:53:51.880 --> 00:53:53.670
experiment going to look like.

00:53:53.670 --> 00:53:57.930
So that's some loop that I do
and what does it look like?

00:53:57.930 --> 00:54:00.040
You can go through it, but basically,

00:54:00.040 --> 00:54:03.140
now you sort of do the same
thing that's on the left,

00:54:03.140 --> 00:54:06.296
but with simulated data that you expect

00:54:06.296 --> 00:54:09.400
that you would see if you
do a certain intervention,

00:54:09.400 --> 00:54:11.500
which you can calculate
'cause you have a simulator.

00:54:11.500 --> 00:54:13.780
But this becomes, there's
like a, it's just very,

00:54:13.780 --> 00:54:15.590
very expensive computationally to do this

00:54:15.590 --> 00:54:18.830
because you have all
this stimulations inside

00:54:18.830 --> 00:54:20.330
of loops inside of loops.

00:54:20.330 --> 00:54:23.020
And so computationally, this is just,

00:54:23.020 --> 00:54:25.170
it's doable, but it's
very, very expensive.

00:54:26.320 --> 00:54:29.501
Now, I prototyped this
with some colleagues

00:54:29.501 --> 00:54:31.580
and we were able to get a system

00:54:31.580 --> 00:54:33.760
that could basically optimize

00:54:33.760 --> 00:54:37.370
what beam energy should you
use for a particle collider

00:54:37.370 --> 00:54:40.030
if you want to measure one
of the particular quantities

00:54:40.030 --> 00:54:42.610
inside of the standard model,

00:54:42.610 --> 00:54:45.383
called the weak mixing
angle, or the Weinberg angle.

00:54:46.923 --> 00:54:49.440
And you can do that,
and it basically says,

00:54:49.440 --> 00:54:51.680
okay, the expected information
gain has a function

00:54:51.680 --> 00:54:54.127
of the center mass energy,
looks something like this.

00:54:54.127 --> 00:54:56.323
And this agrees with what you expect,

00:54:57.505 --> 00:54:59.980
if you've got a PhD in particle physics,

00:54:59.980 --> 00:55:01.840
this is probably some
problem that you had to do

00:55:01.840 --> 00:55:03.540
for homework at some point.

00:55:03.540 --> 00:55:05.680
But what's interesting is
a graduate student can look

00:55:05.680 --> 00:55:08.040
at the equations and can
just reason through it

00:55:08.040 --> 00:55:10.257
without any of this computation and say,

00:55:10.257 --> 00:55:13.087
"Yes, you should run like
at roughly this energy,

00:55:13.087 --> 00:55:14.820
"that's where you're
gonna learn the most."

00:55:14.820 --> 00:55:18.116
And that's exactly what the
experimentalists did, right?

00:55:18.116 --> 00:55:21.250
So, it's cool that you can
operationalize this whole thing

00:55:21.250 --> 00:55:23.650
with machine learning,
but it's very inefficient

00:55:24.763 --> 00:55:27.060
and somehow humans have been able

00:55:27.060 --> 00:55:29.010
to do this much more efficiently.

00:55:29.010 --> 00:55:32.530
So after I put this
together, I tweeted about it,

00:55:32.530 --> 00:55:33.930
Danilo Rezende, who's a well known

00:55:33.930 --> 00:55:36.437
machine learning researcher
was like, "Oh, that's cool.

00:55:36.437 --> 00:55:38.789
"You've got the whole scientific method

00:55:38.789 --> 00:55:40.230
"in a Python notebook."

00:55:40.230 --> 00:55:44.503
And it's half true, but it's
really in the most confined,

00:55:45.740 --> 00:55:47.270
pristine case possible.

00:55:47.270 --> 00:55:48.920
The simulator is well specified,

00:55:48.920 --> 00:55:50.760
the space of experimental configurations

00:55:50.760 --> 00:55:53.860
is very well specified, and
still it was hard to do.

00:55:53.860 --> 00:55:55.420
So I guess what I would
draw attention to is

00:55:55.420 --> 00:55:57.290
if you want to be able
to do something like this

00:55:57.290 --> 00:56:00.010
and like humans do in a open world

00:56:00.010 --> 00:56:01.910
of possible experimental configurations,

00:56:01.910 --> 00:56:04.420
and you also want to include
hypothesis generation

00:56:04.420 --> 00:56:07.700
and things like this,
modern AI is very far away

00:56:07.700 --> 00:56:09.190
from being able to do that.

00:56:09.190 --> 00:56:10.033
But that's interesting, right?

00:56:10.033 --> 00:56:12.513
Like that's the path
that we want to go down.

00:56:14.840 --> 00:56:18.130
Okay, so, yeah, so going back to this kind

00:56:18.130 --> 00:56:21.030
of overall big picture
about the scientific method,

00:56:21.030 --> 00:56:23.550
I think if we want to get to this point

00:56:23.550 --> 00:56:25.900
where we have AI type systems

00:56:25.900 --> 00:56:27.440
that are actually designing experiments,

00:56:27.440 --> 00:56:30.760
formulating hypotheses,
where we're missing some,

00:56:30.760 --> 00:56:33.180
it's not gonna be some
end-to-end learning,

00:56:33.180 --> 00:56:34.910
deep neural network that has no structure,

00:56:34.910 --> 00:56:38.350
it's going to, I think,
need to have components

00:56:38.350 --> 00:56:41.620
that you can point to that
are organized in some way

00:56:41.620 --> 00:56:43.650
that looks like some
kind of causal structure.

00:56:43.650 --> 00:56:46.420
And it may not have the
same kind of ontology

00:56:46.420 --> 00:56:47.550
that current physicists have.

00:56:47.550 --> 00:56:49.640
It may be some foreign ontology.

00:56:49.640 --> 00:56:52.300
But I think it's going to
need to have some ontology

00:56:52.300 --> 00:56:53.500
of different kinds of components

00:56:53.500 --> 00:56:55.823
that's at its essence causal,

00:56:56.778 --> 00:56:57.800
and that it's gonna need to be able

00:56:57.800 --> 00:57:00.863
to manipulate them effectively
if it's going to do

00:57:00.863 --> 00:57:05.110
in a science and any
kind of reasonable way.

00:57:05.110 --> 00:57:07.480
And I think thinking through
that is really important

00:57:07.480 --> 00:57:09.790
for us trying to like put our finger

00:57:09.790 --> 00:57:12.860
on what is it about
causality that's important?

00:57:12.860 --> 00:57:15.230
And to what degree are we kind of,

00:57:15.230 --> 00:57:17.010
should we expect that this,

00:57:17.010 --> 00:57:20.410
like this future AI
system is going to arrive

00:57:20.410 --> 00:57:22.390
at an ontology that
sort of maps one to one

00:57:22.390 --> 00:57:24.640
onto ontology that physicists have

00:57:24.640 --> 00:57:27.130
with electrons and muons and Higgs bosons

00:57:27.130 --> 00:57:28.130
and things like that?

00:57:28.130 --> 00:57:30.490
Maybe not, but it would be kind of a,

00:57:30.490 --> 00:57:31.580
it would be a little bit surprising,

00:57:31.580 --> 00:57:34.380
I would say, if it really didn't,

00:57:34.380 --> 00:57:36.473
if there wasn't some correspondence there.

00:57:37.410 --> 00:57:39.690
Okay, so, yeah, so this was just kind

00:57:39.690 --> 00:57:42.010
of a editorial note
that if you think about

00:57:42.010 --> 00:57:44.230
how humans do this, it's pretty amazing.

00:57:44.230 --> 00:57:46.610
And when I think, when
I introspect unto myself

00:57:46.610 --> 00:57:49.480
as a physicist, I
definitely have roughly five

00:57:49.480 --> 00:57:51.170
or so different mental models

00:57:51.170 --> 00:57:53.820
for experimental particle physics

00:57:53.820 --> 00:57:57.475
that I bounce between at different scales.

00:57:57.475 --> 00:57:59.830
And I choose the one,
(softly coughs) excuse me,

00:57:59.830 --> 00:58:01.870
that's appropriate for a given task

00:58:01.870 --> 00:58:04.010
and that's how I do physical reasoning.

00:58:04.010 --> 00:58:05.850
And I have a very hard time seeing

00:58:05.850 --> 00:58:08.300
how something that's only
working at the low level

00:58:08.300 --> 00:58:10.250
of a simulator with

00:58:11.660 --> 00:58:13.730
tracking the microscopic trajectories

00:58:13.730 --> 00:58:15.910
of particles ionizing
off of atoms is going

00:58:15.910 --> 00:58:19.620
to be able to cope with
the kinds of problems

00:58:19.620 --> 00:58:21.440
that a human does that
can just quickly jump

00:58:21.440 --> 00:58:25.060
to a very like stick-figure-like
picture of what's going on.

00:58:25.060 --> 00:58:27.440
And I think that AI models are gonna need

00:58:27.440 --> 00:58:29.320
to get to that point.

00:58:29.320 --> 00:58:30.580
So in my last couple minutes,

00:58:30.580 --> 00:58:33.420
I'm just gonna flash a
few things that are a view

00:58:33.420 --> 00:58:35.550
from the machine learning and AI community

00:58:35.550 --> 00:58:38.879
to basically try to say that this line

00:58:38.879 --> 00:58:41.670
of thinking is not foreign to the AI

00:58:41.670 --> 00:58:43.330
and machine learning community,

00:58:43.330 --> 00:58:46.463
which to some degree,
I'm a part of myself now.

00:58:47.340 --> 00:58:49.270
But just some concepts
that are floating around

00:58:49.270 --> 00:58:50.103
in that discussion.

00:58:50.103 --> 00:58:52.570
So one of them, there's something called,

00:58:52.570 --> 00:58:55.160
the no free lunch theorem,
which basically says that

00:58:55.160 --> 00:58:57.950
if you don't make any assumptions

00:58:57.950 --> 00:58:59.512
and you imagine all the possible

00:58:59.512 --> 00:59:02.050
like machine learning
problems that you might have,

00:59:02.050 --> 00:59:04.011
like all the possible data distributions

00:59:04.011 --> 00:59:06.560
that essentially there is no,

00:59:06.560 --> 00:59:08.830
every algorithm is going
to perform equally well

00:59:08.830 --> 00:59:10.850
to every other algorithm on average.

00:59:10.850 --> 00:59:13.520
So if one algorithm is
doing better than another,

00:59:13.520 --> 00:59:16.923
it somehow has to be leveraging
something that's atypical.

00:59:18.010 --> 00:59:21.106
And so if you think about, say, images,

00:59:21.106 --> 00:59:22.507
if you don't add any structure,

00:59:22.507 --> 00:59:26.020
something on the left here,
that's the typical image.

00:59:26.020 --> 00:59:27.500
It's just some random noise, right?

00:59:27.500 --> 00:59:30.370
That's what most images look like that.

00:59:30.370 --> 00:59:32.300
But natural images don't
look like that at all.

00:59:32.300 --> 00:59:34.000
They have all sorts of
structure and smoothness

00:59:34.000 --> 00:59:35.610
and all sorts of things going on.

00:59:35.610 --> 00:59:39.300
So the effectiveness of like convolutional

00:59:39.300 --> 00:59:41.040
neural networks and
things has something to do

00:59:41.040 --> 00:59:43.610
with the fact that they're
exploiting the structure

00:59:43.610 --> 00:59:46.430
that's of actual natural images.

00:59:46.430 --> 00:59:49.590
And otherwise, things like
curse of dimensionality,

00:59:49.590 --> 00:59:53.350
you would naively think that
it should basically never work.

00:59:53.350 --> 00:59:55.660
Stephane Mallatt is a
well known researcher,

00:59:55.660 --> 00:59:58.030
did work with wavelets
and things like that.

00:59:58.030 --> 00:59:59.710
So he's talking about learning physics

00:59:59.710 --> 01:00:01.020
with deep neural networks and points

01:00:01.020 --> 01:00:03.440
to two really central pillars,

01:00:03.440 --> 01:00:05.580
if you want to hope
that this is gonna work.

01:00:05.580 --> 01:00:08.010
And one of them is finding symmetries

01:00:08.010 --> 01:00:09.740
and the other one is separating phenomena

01:00:09.740 --> 01:00:10.573
at different scales.

01:00:10.573 --> 01:00:12.680
So we've already seen
that a few different times

01:00:12.680 --> 01:00:14.800
and the convolutional neural networks,

01:00:14.800 --> 01:00:17.673
they incorporate both of these ideas.

01:00:18.688 --> 01:00:22.160
The broader class of these types of ways

01:00:22.160 --> 01:00:25.210
of adding something to the
model so that it will work

01:00:25.210 --> 01:00:27.340
are referred to as inductive bias.

01:00:27.340 --> 01:00:29.920
It's biased in the sense
that it's biasing towards,

01:00:29.920 --> 01:00:32.690
to be effective towards
data of a certain type,

01:00:32.690 --> 01:00:34.900
that has a certain characteristics,

01:00:34.900 --> 01:00:38.150
and it's maybe not gonna
help in other situations.

01:00:38.150 --> 01:00:40.950
So here you see scale separation symmetry,

01:00:40.950 --> 01:00:44.420
the idea of compositionality,
the idea of relationships

01:00:44.420 --> 01:00:46.090
between different objects and causality.

01:00:46.090 --> 01:00:48.120
These are, I think, some
of the most important forms

01:00:48.120 --> 01:00:49.780
of inductive bias.

01:00:49.780 --> 01:00:51.680
You see Peter, like Peter Battaglia,

01:00:51.680 --> 01:00:53.410
who's at DeepMind now and was previously

01:00:53.410 --> 01:00:55.020
with Joshua Tenenbaum and company,

01:00:55.020 --> 01:00:58.230
and has a background in
human and cognitive science,

01:00:58.230 --> 01:01:02.390
talking about model building,
combinatorial generalization

01:01:02.390 --> 01:01:05.200
sort of the infinite use of finite means,

01:01:05.200 --> 01:01:06.990
and about these kinds of ideas

01:01:06.990 --> 01:01:09.160
about intelligence and model building.

01:01:09.160 --> 01:01:11.950
He's taken these ideas to
do really impressive work

01:01:11.950 --> 01:01:13.200
that have to do with like

01:01:14.670 --> 01:01:16.637
you're interested in studying
some physical system,

01:01:16.637 --> 01:01:19.540
and you're gonna come up with
a neural network architecture

01:01:19.540 --> 01:01:22.700
that has some structure associated to it

01:01:22.700 --> 01:01:24.270
that's kind of a mirror of what you think

01:01:24.270 --> 01:01:26.260
the actual physical system looks like.

01:01:26.260 --> 01:01:28.170
I've worked with him on
some of these projects.

01:01:28.170 --> 01:01:30.990
I think it's a very promising direction.

01:01:30.990 --> 01:01:33.930
And then you see Stefanie,

01:01:33.930 --> 01:01:37.680
who's at MIT talking about
a more formal version

01:01:37.680 --> 01:01:39.040
of the same idea.

01:01:39.040 --> 01:01:41.200
She calls it algorithmic alignment,

01:01:41.200 --> 01:01:43.370
and she has some formal
theorems and things.

01:01:43.370 --> 01:01:44.810
But it's some approach to try

01:01:44.810 --> 01:01:47.770
to formalize this idea of
inductive bias, sorry about

01:01:47.770 --> 01:01:50.750
the street noise here.
(sirens softly howling)

01:01:50.750 --> 01:01:53.787
And then finally, this is
Yoshua Bengio talking about,

01:01:53.787 --> 01:01:56.856
"Okay, dear machine learning community,

01:01:56.856 --> 01:01:59.377
"what if we had the right causal structure

01:01:59.377 --> 01:02:01.060
"in our neural networks?"

01:02:01.060 --> 01:02:03.380
And the claim is that basically,

01:02:03.380 --> 01:02:05.890
if you had these kind of
independent mechanisms

01:02:07.299 --> 01:02:09.279
in your causal mechanism,

01:02:09.279 --> 01:02:11.570
that you'll be able to

01:02:12.560 --> 01:02:14.420
learn with many fewer samples,

01:02:14.420 --> 01:02:17.368
that's referred to as sample complexity.

01:02:17.368 --> 01:02:20.530
And that it'll be more
robust to things like

01:02:20.530 --> 01:02:22.050
what's called distribution shift

01:02:22.050 --> 01:02:24.670
or domain shift and things like this.

01:02:24.670 --> 01:02:26.900
The idea that if like
the data that you use

01:02:26.900 --> 01:02:28.750
for training is not exactly
the same as the data

01:02:28.750 --> 01:02:30.820
that you're trying to apply
it to in the real world,

01:02:30.820 --> 01:02:32.260
it'll be more robust.

01:02:32.260 --> 01:02:34.850
And you see various discussion
from other luminaries

01:02:34.850 --> 01:02:36.390
in machine learning.

01:02:36.390 --> 01:02:39.180
So these are very much on people's mind.

01:02:39.180 --> 01:02:41.060
These topics are very
much on people's mind.

01:02:41.060 --> 01:02:43.970
And what I'm interested in is the fact

01:02:43.970 --> 01:02:46.290
that the kind of traditional direction

01:02:46.290 --> 01:02:47.410
that's coming from physics

01:02:47.410 --> 01:02:50.870
and this kind of modern
machine learning thinking

01:02:50.870 --> 01:02:53.600
is coming together at the same time

01:02:53.600 --> 01:02:56.120
and the role of causality
is very important.

01:02:56.120 --> 01:02:59.803
So I already mentioned basically
this idea of trying to,

01:03:00.780 --> 01:03:02.120
the importance of machine learning

01:03:02.120 --> 01:03:04.140
for simulation-based inferences.

01:03:04.140 --> 01:03:06.810
But you also see this idea
that if you can incorporate

01:03:06.810 --> 01:03:08.700
what you know about the
data generating process

01:03:08.700 --> 01:03:11.290
into the machine learning
models themselves,

01:03:11.290 --> 01:03:14.380
that that can basically
help with sample complexity.

01:03:14.380 --> 01:03:17.080
And it's kind of a path towards
how AI might actually start

01:03:17.080 --> 01:03:19.252
to learn the underlying causal mechanism,

01:03:19.252 --> 01:03:22.300
which I think is on the
path towards AI generating

01:03:22.300 --> 01:03:24.463
hypotheses and designing experiments.

01:03:27.479 --> 01:03:30.010
And then lastly, I'd
just say that there's,

01:03:30.010 --> 01:03:32.940
going back to some of
John Woodward's points,

01:03:32.940 --> 01:03:34.430
is that this way of framing it

01:03:34.430 --> 01:03:35.970
and thinking about it allows you

01:03:35.970 --> 01:03:39.050
to basically do experiments where you can,

01:03:39.050 --> 01:03:41.060
the simulators basically,

01:03:41.060 --> 01:03:44.170
they have a ground truth
data generating process

01:03:44.170 --> 01:03:45.290
where you're not arguing about

01:03:45.290 --> 01:03:47.640
what the actual causal story is

01:03:47.640 --> 01:03:48.900
because it's right there in front of you

01:03:48.900 --> 01:03:50.350
in your computer program.

01:03:50.350 --> 01:03:52.750
And then you can look to see what kind of,

01:03:52.750 --> 01:03:55.450
how effective is it to impose this type

01:03:55.450 --> 01:03:57.310
of inductive bias on the model

01:03:57.310 --> 01:04:00.180
and how much does that help in terms

01:04:00.180 --> 01:04:02.610
of these like normative aspects about,

01:04:02.610 --> 01:04:06.210
that bring together causation
and causal reasoning?

01:04:06.210 --> 01:04:08.350
And with that, I will say thank you

01:04:08.350 --> 01:04:13.130
and remind you that next
week on Wednesday at 4:00 PM,

01:04:13.130 --> 01:04:14.960
I have a follow up discussion with Zenna

01:04:14.960 --> 01:04:16.630
and I'm looking forward to that very much.

01:04:16.630 --> 01:04:18.163
Thank you much, very much.

01:04:20.240 --> 01:04:22.230
- Thank you so much, Kyle,

01:04:22.230 --> 01:04:24.693
for that absolutely fantastic talk.

01:04:25.890 --> 01:04:28.080
Very, very clear exposition

01:04:28.080 --> 01:04:31.140
and I really appreciate the
fact that you put effort

01:04:31.140 --> 01:04:34.876
into combining and putting
together the various threads

01:04:34.876 --> 01:04:37.900
that previous speakers and the directions

01:04:37.900 --> 01:04:41.820
that previous speakers
in the series have taken

01:04:41.820 --> 01:04:43.620
to address these issues.

01:04:43.620 --> 01:04:47.630
I personally really appreciated
your showing the differences

01:04:48.700 --> 01:04:51.440
in the meaning of causality,

01:04:51.440 --> 01:04:56.034
the different meanings of
causality that have been used

01:04:56.034 --> 01:04:58.430
and how they actually
relate to each other.

01:04:58.430 --> 01:05:00.970
So there are questions
lining up in the chart,

01:05:00.970 --> 01:05:04.592
but I'm jumping with one question myself.

01:05:04.592 --> 01:05:06.288
So I'm gonna take this

01:05:06.288 --> 01:05:08.488
(laughing drowns out
Priya) first, which is,

01:05:10.468 --> 01:05:15.113
so, I loved your sort of
clarifying the process of inference

01:05:16.000 --> 01:05:19.640
and how in a domain like physics,

01:05:19.640 --> 01:05:24.640
the known laws actually guide
us and how replicating that.

01:05:24.670 --> 01:05:28.400
So I was wondering, so in terms
of new discoveries, right?

01:05:28.400 --> 01:05:33.400
So suppose there's a hitter
to unknown conservation law.

01:05:39.170 --> 01:05:42.400
Because we are moving
using our domain knowledge

01:05:42.400 --> 01:05:44.820
to come up with a suitable
summary statistic,

01:05:44.820 --> 01:05:48.675
and just following the language
of the examples in physics.

01:05:48.675 --> 01:05:51.410
So is it completely impossible,

01:05:51.410 --> 01:05:54.450
because our summary statistic is limited,

01:05:54.450 --> 01:05:56.230
because it's defined entirely

01:05:56.230 --> 01:05:58.820
by what is known, prior knowledge,

01:05:58.820 --> 01:06:01.490
that something that is hitter to unknown

01:06:01.490 --> 01:06:03.387
will actually be mystic.

01:06:03.387 --> 01:06:05.783
How would you capture,
I want to understand

01:06:05.783 --> 01:06:08.930
how you could open up discovery space.

01:06:08.930 --> 01:06:10.270
- Yeah, no, that's a great question.

01:06:10.270 --> 01:06:13.170
So I think, the typical
way that we've gone around,

01:06:13.170 --> 01:06:16.250
gone about designing summary
statistics has been guided

01:06:16.250 --> 01:06:18.940
by how we think the data,

01:06:18.940 --> 01:06:21.780
like our assumptions about
what's actually going on,

01:06:21.780 --> 01:06:23.790
or there's a hypothesis we
don't know if it's true or not.

01:06:23.790 --> 01:06:26.770
But if it were true, it has
the following consequences

01:06:26.770 --> 01:06:30.020
and we can use that as a
guide for designing a summary

01:06:30.020 --> 01:06:32.020
that's going to be very powerful, right?

01:06:32.870 --> 01:06:35.470
But your other approaches that you can use

01:06:35.470 --> 01:06:36.980
to kind of take the hide,

01:06:36.980 --> 01:06:38.410
it's essentially like data compression,

01:06:38.410 --> 01:06:39.578
you have high-dimensional data,

01:06:39.578 --> 01:06:41.270
and you're going to distill it down

01:06:41.270 --> 01:06:43.623
to some lower-dimensional thing.

01:06:44.610 --> 01:06:47.660
And you'll discard some information
on the way, that's fine.

01:06:47.660 --> 01:06:48.493
I mean, these summary statistics

01:06:48.493 --> 01:06:50.680
that we use in particle
physics are good for one task,

01:06:50.680 --> 01:06:52.230
but they're not good for other tasks.

01:06:52.230 --> 01:06:53.740
So it's not surprising that you're

01:06:53.740 --> 01:06:55.610
gonna throw away a lot of data.

01:06:55.610 --> 01:06:58.910
But there are certainly
approaches where you try to

01:07:00.850 --> 01:07:03.240
sort of learn, in machine learning,

01:07:03.240 --> 01:07:05.510
they would be referred to as
a representation of the data.

01:07:05.510 --> 01:07:07.490
You have the full data,

01:07:07.490 --> 01:07:08.890
and then you have some representation

01:07:08.890 --> 01:07:11.230
that will capture some
pieces of information,

01:07:11.230 --> 01:07:12.480
but not others, right?

01:07:12.480 --> 01:07:15.750
And so, there are all sorts of approaches,

01:07:15.750 --> 01:07:17.610
including unsupervised learning,

01:07:17.610 --> 01:07:19.879
or even what I was talking about

01:07:19.879 --> 01:07:22.930
and what I showed with where
we are using neural network.

01:07:22.930 --> 01:07:24.690
That neural network
surrogate, in some senses,

01:07:24.690 --> 01:07:27.130
is a learned summary statistic.

01:07:27.130 --> 01:07:28.430
But it's, again, it's learned

01:07:28.430 --> 01:07:31.010
because it's targeting
a specific hypothesis.

01:07:31.010 --> 01:07:33.270
If you wanted to do it
in a way that's like,

01:07:33.270 --> 01:07:34.520
I think the essence of your question

01:07:34.520 --> 01:07:36.380
where you're not assuming the hypothesis,

01:07:36.380 --> 01:07:37.430
but you're trying to learn from data,

01:07:37.430 --> 01:07:38.928
what would that look like?

01:07:38.928 --> 01:07:40.700
And there certainly are various approaches

01:07:40.700 --> 01:07:42.340
that are coming out now,

01:07:42.340 --> 01:07:44.650
things that are related
to like anomaly detection

01:07:44.650 --> 01:07:45.700
and stuff like that where you try

01:07:45.700 --> 01:07:48.300
to essentially learn some representation,

01:07:48.300 --> 01:07:51.250
which is good at capturing what
most of the data looks like

01:07:51.250 --> 01:07:53.620
and then you look for something unusual

01:07:53.620 --> 01:07:55.913
in some sense of unusual.

01:07:57.700 --> 01:08:02.040
There's progress there but
it's a bit of a can of worms.

01:08:02.040 --> 01:08:06.260
If you wanted to discover
something like a symmetry,

01:08:06.260 --> 01:08:10.050
I think there are definitely
works there to try to,

01:08:10.050 --> 01:08:11.600
how could you discover them?

01:08:11.600 --> 01:08:15.050
One of them is you basically
sort of enumerate a bunch

01:08:15.050 --> 01:08:16.976
of possibilities and then you basically--

01:08:16.976 --> 01:08:18.284
- You brute-force it, right?
- It turns into,

01:08:18.284 --> 01:08:20.583
Yeah.
- Yeah, you brute-force it,

01:08:20.583 --> 01:08:25.583
but, you have to still
start with some prior

01:08:26.270 --> 01:08:28.810
expectation, maybe it's even as silly

01:08:28.810 --> 01:08:30.710
as dimensional analysis
or something, right?

01:08:30.710 --> 01:08:34.243
But you have to just brute-force
through possibilities.

01:08:38.008 --> 01:08:39.060
That's pretty clear.

01:08:39.060 --> 01:08:41.180
So, hey, let me move on
to the first question,

01:08:41.180 --> 01:08:43.477
which is somewhat
related, it's from Ferdos.

01:08:43.477 --> 01:08:45.217
"What if we want to look at physics

01:08:45.217 --> 01:08:46.997
"beyond the standard model?

01:08:46.997 --> 01:08:50.477
"Where the theory is
an uncharted territory

01:08:50.477 --> 01:08:51.437
"in a manner of speaking.

01:08:51.437 --> 01:08:54.100
"So how do we hunt for these signatures?"

01:08:54.100 --> 01:08:54.950
- Yeah, no, exactly.

01:08:54.950 --> 01:08:57.800
So I think the, it's a
question that comes up a lot

01:08:57.800 --> 01:08:59.707
in this talk, which is like,
"Well, that's great and all,

01:08:59.707 --> 01:09:03.027
"but I wanna learn something
that's beyond my simulator,

01:09:03.027 --> 01:09:04.700
"like, I wanna learn new physics."

01:09:04.700 --> 01:09:07.480
So, one thing that's
important to realize is

01:09:07.480 --> 01:09:09.560
that we don't just have a simulator

01:09:09.560 --> 01:09:11.320
for like the standard model.

01:09:11.320 --> 01:09:15.570
We have simulators for basically
all of the crazy thoughts

01:09:15.570 --> 01:09:19.060
that theorists have come up
with over the last decades.

01:09:19.060 --> 01:09:24.060
So we have a formalism that's
kind of general purpose.

01:09:24.470 --> 01:09:26.520
And we describe, essentially,

01:09:26.520 --> 01:09:29.550
every new physics theory
that we can think of

01:09:29.550 --> 01:09:31.520
gets described in that formalism.

01:09:31.520 --> 01:09:33.390
And the tools that we have can allow us

01:09:33.390 --> 01:09:34.910
to make predictions of what
we would expect the data

01:09:34.910 --> 01:09:37.330
to look like for kind of any theory

01:09:37.330 --> 01:09:39.490
in that space of theories.

01:09:39.490 --> 01:09:42.140
Now you could say, "I want
to go even beyond that,"

01:09:42.140 --> 01:09:45.310
and ask about theories
that are not expressed

01:09:45.310 --> 01:09:46.210
in that formalism.

01:09:48.426 --> 01:09:50.810
And then, I guess what to say,

01:09:50.810 --> 01:09:52.970
essentially, all kinds
of inferences are going

01:09:52.970 --> 01:09:55.820
to be done at some point in
the context of a model, right?

01:09:55.820 --> 01:09:57.250
So you need some notion of saying,

01:09:57.250 --> 01:09:59.730
what do you think the data
is going to look like?

01:09:59.730 --> 01:10:02.050
And that doesn't have to be expressed

01:10:02.050 --> 01:10:03.050
in the language of quantum field theory.

01:10:03.050 --> 01:10:07.000
It could be a very
empirically motivated way,

01:10:07.000 --> 01:10:08.290
like signature based where actually,

01:10:08.290 --> 01:10:09.520
like, "What if there's a bump in the data?

01:10:09.520 --> 01:10:11.630
"What if the data does this?"

01:10:11.630 --> 01:10:13.100
It doesn't have to be based

01:10:13.100 --> 01:10:14.630
on quantum field theory or something.

01:10:14.630 --> 01:10:16.357
And so then you can say,

01:10:16.357 --> 01:10:17.937
"Well, I don't know exactly
what the bump looks like,

01:10:17.937 --> 01:10:21.930
"but can you search for any
bump or any sharp feature?"

01:10:21.930 --> 01:10:23.797
Or, "Does it ever have any kind of,"

01:10:24.650 --> 01:10:26.960
if you don't say, add
some other assumption,

01:10:26.960 --> 01:10:30.230
you just say anything other
than the standard model,

01:10:30.230 --> 01:10:33.180
then you open yourself up
to a bunch of possibilities

01:10:33.180 --> 01:10:34.660
that people don't wanna take seriously,

01:10:34.660 --> 01:10:37.090
like electronic noise in your detector

01:10:37.090 --> 01:10:39.110
that has no kind of structure to it,

01:10:39.110 --> 01:10:41.137
that no one, no physicists
would look and be like,

01:10:41.137 --> 01:10:41.970
"Ooh, new physics."

01:10:41.970 --> 01:10:43.960
They'd just be like, "I've
got a cable that's loose,"

01:10:43.960 --> 01:10:45.787
or something, you know?

01:10:45.787 --> 01:10:47.743
But trying to figure
out how to balance that

01:10:47.743 --> 01:10:52.030
between very well specified
theories that are so narrow

01:10:52.030 --> 01:10:53.440
that they maybe seem uninteresting

01:10:53.440 --> 01:10:55.233
and a completely open ended,

01:10:56.430 --> 01:10:58.747
could be anything where you have no power,

01:10:58.747 --> 01:11:01.670
finding that trade-off is challenging.

01:11:01.670 --> 01:11:03.873
And so coming up with a
way to describe theories

01:11:03.873 --> 01:11:06.730
in some kind of effective way
that people are happy with

01:11:06.730 --> 01:11:07.980
that maintains some power

01:11:09.306 --> 01:11:10.930
is kind of a transition that's happening.

01:11:10.930 --> 01:11:13.000
Well, I mean, it's an old problem.

01:11:13.000 --> 01:11:15.970
It's a classic problem in
statistics, but it's renewed.

01:11:15.970 --> 01:11:17.440
It has renewed energy in the context

01:11:17.440 --> 01:11:18.640
of machine learning now.

01:11:19.540 --> 01:11:21.360
- Thank you very much.

01:11:21.360 --> 01:11:25.117
The next question is
from Li Hao, Li Hao Yung.

01:11:25.117 --> 01:11:27.017
"In my understanding, the state-of-the-art

01:11:27.017 --> 01:11:30.387
"machine learning algorithms
really act like a black box.

01:11:30.387 --> 01:11:31.997
"It's very difficult to interpret

01:11:31.997 --> 01:11:34.787
"why the algorithm actually works.

01:11:34.787 --> 01:11:38.327
"Would this reduce the
power of ML inferences?"

01:11:39.848 --> 01:11:42.420
- By keeping it a black box

01:11:42.420 --> 01:11:44.950
or by making something that's not a,

01:11:44.950 --> 01:11:46.910
this meaning what I'm preferring?

01:11:46.910 --> 01:11:47.743
- [Priya] Yeah.

01:11:47.743 --> 01:11:51.060
- Well, I think that, yeah,
so let's see what to say.

01:11:51.060 --> 01:11:53.640
So, that general idea of adding structure

01:11:53.640 --> 01:11:56.720
to your architecture or
your model was referred to

01:11:56.720 --> 01:11:58.330
as inductive bias, right?

01:11:58.330 --> 01:12:00.120
So why is that term used?

01:12:00.120 --> 01:12:02.270
Inductive, because you're
kind of adding some structure

01:12:02.270 --> 01:12:05.620
to the model to try to, 'cause you're,

01:12:05.620 --> 01:12:09.180
but the bias part is
because you're not giving,

01:12:09.180 --> 01:12:10.960
you're not saying as
an equal playing field

01:12:10.960 --> 01:12:12.440
to all possible scenarios

01:12:12.440 --> 01:12:14.170
that the data could look like anything.

01:12:14.170 --> 01:12:16.797
You're saying, "I think
the data has some kind of,

01:12:16.797 --> 01:12:18.040
"is organized in some way."

01:12:18.040 --> 01:12:21.330
It has some type of structure
and when you impose that,

01:12:21.330 --> 01:12:23.850
then you are kind of,
you're favoring yourself

01:12:23.850 --> 01:12:24.978
in that direction.

01:12:24.978 --> 01:12:27.120
And so you're kind of
biasing yourself in that way.

01:12:27.120 --> 01:12:31.320
Now, if the thing, if reality
actually has that structure,

01:12:31.320 --> 01:12:33.130
then you're kind of, you're fine.

01:12:33.130 --> 01:12:34.960
Like if the reality is there,

01:12:34.960 --> 01:12:38.298
if reality's not quite
in that family of things,

01:12:38.298 --> 01:12:40.860
then maybe you have a bit of a problem.

01:12:40.860 --> 01:12:44.020
But generally, if you
can impose that structure

01:12:44.020 --> 01:12:45.990
and it's like the right
thing, you're good.

01:12:45.990 --> 01:12:47.440
Of course you don't know
if it's right or not.

01:12:47.440 --> 01:12:50.250
So it's a tough question
to answer it generally.

01:12:50.250 --> 01:12:54.420
But the other thing to say is
if you think about situations,

01:12:54.420 --> 01:12:56.010
like there have been papers,

01:12:56.010 --> 01:12:57.300
I referred to some of the work

01:12:57.300 --> 01:12:59.100
by Peter Battaglia and company.

01:12:59.100 --> 01:13:02.290
And there's a recent
paper by Miles Cranmer,

01:13:02.290 --> 01:13:05.570
no relationship, who's
looking at, for instance,

01:13:05.570 --> 01:13:07.620
planets moving around and trying to infer

01:13:08.461 --> 01:13:10.480
what is the gravitational law?

01:13:10.480 --> 01:13:12.550
And we've done some work like that.

01:13:12.550 --> 01:13:14.770
Those kinds of models you can use

01:13:14.770 --> 01:13:15.800
what are called graph neural network

01:13:15.800 --> 01:13:18.620
where the nodes of the graph are things

01:13:18.620 --> 01:13:19.810
that have physical meaning,

01:13:19.810 --> 01:13:22.210
like planets or particles or atoms.

01:13:22.210 --> 01:13:24.530
And the edges between
them have physical meaning

01:13:24.530 --> 01:13:26.547
like force between two planets
or something like that.

01:13:26.547 --> 01:13:29.970
And in that sense, you're
kind of doing the hard work

01:13:29.970 --> 01:13:31.220
for the machine learning.

01:13:32.057 --> 01:13:33.540
The machine learning algorithm didn't know

01:13:33.540 --> 01:13:35.450
what planets were or that it wasn't some

01:13:35.450 --> 01:13:37.770
like linear combination of
planets that should be a node.

01:13:37.770 --> 01:13:39.780
It said like each planet should be a node

01:13:39.780 --> 01:13:42.130
and there should be a one-way,

01:13:42.130 --> 01:13:43.970
bidirectional relationship
that's symmetric

01:13:43.970 --> 01:13:45.110
and all sorts of things.

01:13:45.110 --> 01:13:47.610
And so you impose all that inducted bias,

01:13:47.610 --> 01:13:49.560
but in that problem, you think it's right.

01:13:49.560 --> 01:13:52.090
And if you do that, it just
helps tremendously, right?

01:13:52.090 --> 01:13:54.774
If you take that away, you
could in principle get there,

01:13:54.774 --> 01:13:57.060
but you're gonna need a lot more data

01:13:57.060 --> 01:13:58.963
before you perform well.

01:14:00.010 --> 01:14:01.955
- Great, thank you.

01:14:01.955 --> 01:14:06.453
And there's of course a really
profound question from Brian.

01:14:07.617 --> 01:14:10.027
"Thanks for this really fun
and accessible talk, Kyle.

01:14:10.027 --> 01:14:11.447
"Could you talk a little bit about

01:14:11.447 --> 01:14:13.577
"what you've been thinking
regarding the implications

01:14:13.577 --> 01:14:16.787
"for epistemology and the
limits of human knowledge

01:14:16.787 --> 01:14:18.597
"or information acquisition?"

01:14:20.287 --> 01:14:22.140
- Yeah, that's a good one.

01:14:22.140 --> 01:14:24.190
Yeah, I mean, I think, well, what to say?

01:14:25.990 --> 01:14:27.800
I'm not like a trained philosopher,

01:14:27.800 --> 01:14:29.310
so probably everything I will say

01:14:29.310 --> 01:14:31.840
will sound like naive and et cetera.

01:14:31.840 --> 01:14:36.080
But I do often like to
think of it in terms of

01:14:36.080 --> 01:14:39.295
if some alien civilization
came and landed,

01:14:39.295 --> 01:14:41.680
it would be clear that they are,

01:14:41.680 --> 01:14:43.250
if they have the fancy
spaceships and stuff,

01:14:43.250 --> 01:14:45.100
it's clear they have advanced technology.

01:14:45.100 --> 01:14:46.360
You're not gonna be arguing about

01:14:46.360 --> 01:14:47.860
if they're intelligent or not.

01:14:49.532 --> 01:14:51.630
And it's clear that they probably

01:14:51.630 --> 01:14:52.810
have some notion of science

01:14:52.810 --> 01:14:55.330
or they probably wouldn't
have gotten there.

01:14:55.330 --> 01:14:58.920
And so compared to other areas where,

01:14:58.920 --> 01:15:02.620
like with AI, human-level AI,
where you're arguing about

01:15:02.620 --> 01:15:03.820
what does it mean to be intelligent,

01:15:03.820 --> 01:15:07.100
it's like this starting, this
framing, you're kind of not,

01:15:07.100 --> 01:15:09.910
it's a very understandable
notion for I think most people

01:15:09.910 --> 01:15:11.050
that, yes, they're intelligent,

01:15:11.050 --> 01:15:12.850
they've got some notion of science.

01:15:12.850 --> 01:15:15.440
And then you imagine starting
some conversation with them,

01:15:15.440 --> 01:15:19.567
you don't have aligned
language, it's not "Star Trek."

01:15:21.175 --> 01:15:23.510
But say you can figure that part out.

01:15:23.510 --> 01:15:25.070
Then there's gonna be a question of like,

01:15:25.070 --> 01:15:28.150
does the ontology that they have align

01:15:28.150 --> 01:15:30.570
with ontology that we have at all?

01:15:30.570 --> 01:15:32.220
Or do they have like a
totally different way

01:15:32.220 --> 01:15:34.180
of thinking about everything?

01:15:34.180 --> 01:15:35.763
How did that come to be?

01:15:37.870 --> 01:15:40.410
And I think that's a, it's
an interesting question.

01:15:40.410 --> 01:15:43.090
It's not clear, and then when you then use

01:15:43.090 --> 01:15:44.500
that way of thinking about it

01:15:44.500 --> 01:15:48.520
with the conversation towards
like robot scientists and AI,

01:15:48.520 --> 01:15:52.970
like, is there any expectation
that some AI type thing,

01:15:52.970 --> 01:15:55.440
should it be interpretable to humans?

01:15:55.440 --> 01:15:56.850
Well, I don't know,

01:15:56.850 --> 01:15:58.660
as long as humans are in the process

01:15:58.660 --> 01:16:01.900
of designing experiments and
doing hypothesis generation,

01:16:01.900 --> 01:16:04.230
it's pretty important
that we can understand

01:16:04.230 --> 01:16:06.860
what happens for the current
experiment that we're doing.

01:16:06.860 --> 01:16:08.760
And if we somehow seed that

01:16:08.760 --> 01:16:12.180
and have like really black
box neural networks and things

01:16:12.180 --> 01:16:13.510
that maybe solve the probably

01:16:13.510 --> 01:16:15.187
we're on today, really, really well,

01:16:15.187 --> 01:16:18.530
but if something goes wrong,
we don't know what to do next,

01:16:18.530 --> 01:16:21.040
then the whole way in which
we aggregate knowledge

01:16:21.040 --> 01:16:23.580
in the scientific enterprise up until then

01:16:23.580 --> 01:16:26.810
is gonna be kind of dramatically affected.

01:16:26.810 --> 01:16:29.420
And I think that would
probably not go well.

01:16:29.420 --> 01:16:32.430
But I do see, and you're
already starting to see it

01:16:32.430 --> 01:16:35.670
in like self-driving labs for
chemistry and stuff like that.

01:16:35.670 --> 01:16:37.700
You have kind of closed loop systems,

01:16:37.700 --> 01:16:40.549
usually only in pretty confined settings

01:16:40.549 --> 01:16:41.953
where that's effective.

01:16:43.588 --> 01:16:45.990
But yeah, I think it's
a fascinating question

01:16:47.000 --> 01:16:49.300
and I don't know that I
really answered it, but.

01:16:49.300 --> 01:16:53.390
- No, a sort of a related
a thread that occurs to me

01:16:54.280 --> 01:16:57.440
from what you've said, Kyle,

01:16:57.440 --> 01:17:01.340
is it domain knowledge in many ways

01:17:02.330 --> 01:17:05.000
is traded off with the
amount of data, right?

01:17:05.000 --> 01:17:07.140
So if you don't have domain knowledge,

01:17:07.140 --> 01:17:09.430
I mean, is it really true

01:17:09.430 --> 01:17:12.640
that having an infinite
amount of data means

01:17:12.640 --> 01:17:15.223
that you don't actually
need domain knowledge?

01:17:16.260 --> 01:17:17.380
- Oh, yeah, so that's interesting.

01:17:17.380 --> 01:17:19.270
Well, one thing that, I mentioned earlier

01:17:19.270 --> 01:17:21.638
this like ladder of causation.

01:17:21.638 --> 01:17:24.650
Even if you have an
infinite amount of data

01:17:24.650 --> 01:17:26.895
and you have like
infinite computing power,

01:17:26.895 --> 01:17:30.530
and you can basically,
any probabilistic question

01:17:30.530 --> 01:17:34.830
that you want to answer,
but that can be expressed

01:17:34.830 --> 01:17:36.750
as like a joint distribution or a marginal

01:17:36.750 --> 01:17:40.210
or something like that, you
have access to all of that.

01:17:40.210 --> 01:17:41.910
You can do all sorts of things,

01:17:41.910 --> 01:17:44.840
but you can only answer
questions in the lowest rung

01:17:44.840 --> 01:17:46.410
of the causal hierarchy.

01:17:46.410 --> 01:17:49.250
If you ever want to ask, "What
would happen if I intervene,"

01:17:49.250 --> 01:17:52.170
you're just not gonna be able
to answer that on principle.

01:17:52.170 --> 01:17:55.850
So, yeah, so I don't think,
that's why I don't think data,

01:17:55.850 --> 01:17:58.480
like this whole data-driven thing--

01:17:58.480 --> 01:17:59.830
- Yes.
- Is kind of like kind

01:17:59.830 --> 01:18:02.145
of missing a pretty important point.

01:18:02.145 --> 01:18:03.370
- Yeah, right, I mean, this
is where I was going, right?

01:18:03.370 --> 01:18:05.170
Because in our fields, for example,

01:18:05.170 --> 01:18:07.869
in astronomy, cosmology, particle physics,

01:18:07.869 --> 01:18:11.420
I mean, we are really
hurdling towards the,

01:18:11.420 --> 01:18:13.540
just procuring huge amounts of data,

01:18:13.540 --> 01:18:15.900
somehow thinking that that is going

01:18:15.900 --> 01:18:18.460
to be sort of the resolution

01:18:18.460 --> 01:18:20.920
in providing deeper
insights into the theory,

01:18:20.920 --> 01:18:22.890
because there are limits,
there are limits that--

01:18:22.890 --> 01:18:24.640
- Yeah, I mean, it generally helps.

01:18:26.841 --> 01:18:29.910
At the LHC, people use this term,

01:18:29.910 --> 01:18:32.260
data-driven analysis techniques a lot.

01:18:32.260 --> 01:18:35.090
What's interesting is if you look there,

01:18:35.090 --> 01:18:36.420
a lot of times what happens is

01:18:36.420 --> 01:18:38.840
that there's some clever
insight about like

01:18:38.840 --> 01:18:42.250
how you can reuse the data
that has some characteristics

01:18:42.250 --> 01:18:44.860
and then sort of use it to extrapolate

01:18:44.860 --> 01:18:47.230
to what it would look like
in a different setting.

01:18:47.230 --> 01:18:49.160
But the only way that
you're able to do that is

01:18:49.160 --> 01:18:51.817
because we think, "Oh, well,
if I take out the electron

01:18:51.817 --> 01:18:53.197
"and replace it with a muon--
- Muon.

01:18:53.197 --> 01:18:55.610
- "And then I have this
following relationship,"

01:18:55.610 --> 01:18:56.730
and you assume everything else

01:18:56.730 --> 01:18:58.560
is not unchanged and all sorts of stuff.

01:18:58.560 --> 01:19:01.440
So that looks a lot like causality.

01:19:01.440 --> 01:19:05.090
All of those kinds of
operations are kind of

01:19:06.070 --> 01:19:09.150
using causal structure that
physicists have learned

01:19:09.150 --> 01:19:10.757
to do this and those are the--

01:19:10.757 --> 01:19:12.263
(crosstalk drowns out Kyle)
- Preexisting knowledge

01:19:12.263 --> 01:19:13.673
of causal structures, right.

01:19:13.673 --> 01:19:14.790
- But we don't usually
describe it in those language.

01:19:14.790 --> 01:19:16.010
We don't talk, we don't use the language

01:19:16.010 --> 01:19:18.320
of causal inference when we do it.

01:19:18.320 --> 01:19:20.520
- I think Sean has an urgent hand up.

01:19:20.520 --> 01:19:23.020
There are many more
questions, but did you want to

01:19:25.030 --> 01:19:29.420
speak up or I think you may
have to type in the chat, Sean.

01:19:29.420 --> 01:19:31.483
Because we are in the webinar format.

01:19:33.500 --> 01:19:35.120
- [Ty] And also, Priya,

01:19:35.120 --> 01:19:38.190
Zenna has contributed
something in the chat.

01:19:38.190 --> 01:19:39.770
I think because of his host privileges,

01:19:39.770 --> 01:19:42.353
he wasn't able to go into the Q&amp;A.

01:19:43.566 --> 01:19:46.816
- Oh, I see.
- Do you see his question?

01:19:48.610 --> 01:19:49.843
- I see the question, I don't know

01:19:49.843 --> 01:19:51.097
if you see it.
- Yeah, Zenna,

01:19:51.097 --> 01:19:51.947
go ahead and ask.

01:19:59.250 --> 01:20:01.770
I think you can unmute yourself.

01:20:01.770 --> 01:20:03.220
- I can maybe, well, we can just read it.

01:20:03.220 --> 01:20:05.417
So he says, "A question, if I may.

01:20:05.417 --> 01:20:07.290
"You mentioned that
reality seems to striated

01:20:07.290 --> 01:20:08.787
"so that it can be understood

01:20:08.787 --> 01:20:10.847
"at different levels of abstraction.

01:20:10.847 --> 01:20:12.167
"Could it be instead that this is more

01:20:12.167 --> 01:20:14.427
"about human minds than the world?

01:20:14.427 --> 01:20:16.487
"And more pragmatically,
do you have thoughts

01:20:16.487 --> 01:20:18.977
"of model representation
that could capture

01:20:18.977 --> 01:20:21.570
"multiple different
abstractions simultaneously?"

01:20:21.570 --> 01:20:23.370
Yeah, that's a great question.

01:20:23.370 --> 01:20:25.003
Now, I mean, when you get into,

01:20:26.080 --> 01:20:28.080
I mean, this is a super
philosophical question.

01:20:28.080 --> 01:20:30.180
I mean, there's a like,

01:20:30.180 --> 01:20:32.440
there's a question like to
the extent there are cells

01:20:32.440 --> 01:20:36.660
and organs and individual, and then like

01:20:36.660 --> 01:20:39.640
a populations of some species.

01:20:39.640 --> 01:20:42.476
It's, to me, I guess I'm
a bit of a realist there

01:20:42.476 --> 01:20:46.033
in that striation seems to be pretty real.

01:20:48.430 --> 01:20:51.070
In a physics context,
you can go between things

01:20:51.070 --> 01:20:56.070
like quarks to things like
protons and neutrons to atoms,

01:20:56.110 --> 01:21:00.130
and it could be that
that's all an illusion.

01:21:00.130 --> 01:21:03.850
But it's certainly, a
very effective description

01:21:03.850 --> 01:21:06.770
has worked across wildly
different kinds of experiments.

01:21:06.770 --> 01:21:10.660
So it's a very effective abstraction.

01:21:10.660 --> 01:21:13.760
And again, I'm certainly
a bit of a realist there,

01:21:13.760 --> 01:21:18.690
but like, I'm open to
the fact that it's that,

01:21:18.690 --> 01:21:20.940
the broader philosophical
discussions there.

01:21:20.940 --> 01:21:23.283
But in a practical sense,

01:21:25.230 --> 01:21:27.730
whether those things
are really real or not,

01:21:27.730 --> 01:21:31.148
the extent that humans use them as a tool

01:21:31.148 --> 01:21:34.700
to make predictions about
things has certainly,

01:21:34.700 --> 01:21:37.410
empirically were served as very well

01:21:37.410 --> 01:21:38.643
in navigating the world.

01:21:39.946 --> 01:21:42.683
And we have success after
success after success,

01:21:43.586 --> 01:21:46.780
and using that, those
relationships to organize things.

01:21:46.780 --> 01:21:51.329
And I think individual humans,
it certainly seems to me,

01:21:51.329 --> 01:21:53.690
I'm not a cognitive scientist,

01:21:53.690 --> 01:21:56.759
but that we, you read, well,

01:21:56.759 --> 01:21:57.713
many cognitive scientists are saying is

01:21:57.713 --> 01:22:00.338
that this does seem to be how
we're organizing the world

01:22:00.338 --> 01:22:03.000
and that our success as
a species has a lot to do

01:22:03.000 --> 01:22:06.150
with being able to kind of pick
the appropriate abstraction

01:22:06.150 --> 01:22:09.950
and do reasoning very
efficiently using it.

01:22:09.950 --> 01:22:12.140
I think then the last part
of your question to me

01:22:12.140 --> 01:22:15.387
is the part that's the most
compelling currently is

01:22:15.387 --> 01:22:17.150
how do we build that into some kind

01:22:17.150 --> 01:22:19.730
of AI machine-learning-like architecture?

01:22:19.730 --> 01:22:21.670
I think in the context of,

01:22:21.670 --> 01:22:23.410
step one is how do you do the first,

01:22:23.410 --> 01:22:27.290
even before different layers,
at one particular layer,

01:22:27.290 --> 01:22:29.270
how do you include that bias?

01:22:29.270 --> 01:22:30.670
And I think that's where you see things

01:22:30.670 --> 01:22:33.590
like graph neural networks
for certain things

01:22:33.590 --> 01:22:35.130
where you can really point
to individual things,

01:22:35.130 --> 01:22:37.500
like planets and stuff like that.

01:22:37.500 --> 01:22:40.970
It's very clear how to do
it and it works really well.

01:22:40.970 --> 01:22:43.470
A research direction that I
think is interesting is like,

01:22:43.470 --> 01:22:45.870
what if you didn't do that by hand?

01:22:45.870 --> 01:22:47.030
How hard would it be to,

01:22:47.030 --> 01:22:49.920
like if you took the data and
reorganized it in some way,

01:22:49.920 --> 01:22:51.970
and then you said,
okay, you want some meta

01:22:51.970 --> 01:22:53.700
machine learning algorithm to figure out,

01:22:53.700 --> 01:22:57.170
like, what are the objects
in your ontology to attach

01:22:57.170 --> 01:22:59.500
to nodes in a graph and
what the edges mean?

01:22:59.500 --> 01:23:02.870
And how you would do that
in some more automated way.

01:23:02.870 --> 01:23:04.930
That's interesting, there's
work that's going on

01:23:04.930 --> 01:23:07.010
about dynamically building
graph neural networks,

01:23:07.010 --> 01:23:08.390
which is in that direction.

01:23:08.390 --> 01:23:10.650
And then there's the idea
of if you have a graph,

01:23:10.650 --> 01:23:13.360
like coarse-graining
it so that you have a,

01:23:13.360 --> 01:23:16.120
and that looks, it looks sort
of like the pooling operations

01:23:16.120 --> 01:23:18.110
you see in a convolutional neural network.

01:23:18.110 --> 01:23:19.680
So there are certainly ideas

01:23:19.680 --> 01:23:21.250
and there are people exploring that.

01:23:21.250 --> 01:23:24.880
And I have, trying to do some
work like that on my own.

01:23:24.880 --> 01:23:29.230
And I think it's, that's
super, super interesting.

01:23:29.230 --> 01:23:31.650
And there are various
people playing around

01:23:31.650 --> 01:23:33.120
in that area right now.

01:23:33.120 --> 01:23:34.550
- Yeah, no, I mean, Zenna

01:23:34.550 --> 01:23:37.640
this is something we
hope to talk more about

01:23:38.610 --> 01:23:41.760
during your presentation
on next Wednesday.

01:23:41.760 --> 01:23:43.420
But Kyle, related to this,

01:23:43.420 --> 01:23:47.110
I have this little aside
related, which is, I mean,

01:23:47.110 --> 01:23:50.640
isn't it true that when we
are coarse-graining a system

01:23:50.640 --> 01:23:54.020
in physics, for example,
it's very intentional, right?

01:23:54.020 --> 01:23:56.230
I mean, we are coarse-graining
because we know

01:23:56.230 --> 01:23:58.810
that at a certain level of abstraction,

01:23:58.810 --> 01:24:02.010
we will have additional
information that we can use,

01:24:02.010 --> 01:24:04.620
additional priors that can play into,

01:24:04.620 --> 01:24:07.900
we know a new set of
rules, a new distribution,

01:24:07.900 --> 01:24:11.760
a form for a distribution that will work

01:24:11.760 --> 01:24:13.480
for the ensemble, right?

01:24:13.480 --> 01:24:15.380
So the coarse-graining is always

01:24:15.380 --> 01:24:20.000
because we do already have
prior information about

01:24:21.450 --> 01:24:24.320
how a system will look when we...

01:24:25.590 --> 01:24:27.690
- I would say yes and no.

01:24:27.690 --> 01:24:30.420
I think that there's, I would say that,

01:24:30.420 --> 01:24:32.330
and if you look at major
advances in science,

01:24:32.330 --> 01:24:34.050
a lot of times it's like the fact

01:24:34.050 --> 01:24:37.260
that you're able to first identify

01:24:37.260 --> 01:24:40.610
here is a scale where there is an effect,

01:24:40.610 --> 01:24:42.510
there is going to be a useful description,

01:24:42.510 --> 01:24:43.690
and there's gonna be some kind of laws

01:24:43.690 --> 01:24:44.967
that describe what's going on.

01:24:44.967 --> 01:24:46.960
And then you need to kind
of have some ontology

01:24:46.960 --> 01:24:48.680
of what are the right degrees of freedom

01:24:48.680 --> 01:24:49.710
or the right kinds of objects

01:24:49.710 --> 01:24:51.620
to even talk about at that scale?

01:24:51.620 --> 01:24:53.240
And then what do their
interactions look like?

01:24:53.240 --> 01:24:55.327
And then what are the laws
that describe that scale?

01:24:55.327 --> 01:24:58.240
And that, sometimes that just
happens kind of on its own,

01:24:58.240 --> 01:24:59.410
at that level.

01:24:59.410 --> 01:25:01.850
When those things happen,
they're really important, right?

01:25:01.850 --> 01:25:03.710
Those are like major, major

01:25:03.710 --> 01:25:05.720
events in science, right?
- Breakthroughs, major, yeah.

01:25:05.720 --> 01:25:08.440
- And then if you can, and in
the reductionist type picture,

01:25:08.440 --> 01:25:11.837
if you can then tie it back
to the lower-level scale

01:25:11.837 --> 01:25:15.010
and say like, this is how they're related,

01:25:15.010 --> 01:25:17.030
that's even like, that's
a another, I mean,

01:25:17.030 --> 01:25:18.290
these are all like Nobel Prize--

01:25:18.290 --> 01:25:20.570
- Right, right.
- Type of events.

01:25:20.570 --> 01:25:23.130
And so part of what I think is so profound

01:25:23.130 --> 01:25:25.410
about the simulation-based
inference thing is

01:25:25.410 --> 01:25:27.660
that you start off, it's
kind of allows you a way

01:25:27.660 --> 01:25:29.300
to maybe bootstrap that process.

01:25:29.300 --> 01:25:31.430
You start with a simulator at one scale,

01:25:31.430 --> 01:25:33.090
you know what's going on.

01:25:33.090 --> 01:25:34.820
Then there are some emergent phenomena

01:25:34.820 --> 01:25:35.880
that are really complicated

01:25:35.880 --> 01:25:37.220
and you don't know what they are.

01:25:37.220 --> 01:25:39.440
But through learning, you're able to find

01:25:39.440 --> 01:25:43.330
kind of what are the right
summaries, coarse-graining,

01:25:43.330 --> 01:25:47.680
whatever that kind of identify
the new degrees of freedom.

01:25:47.680 --> 01:25:50.050
You don't have a name for them yet.

01:25:50.050 --> 01:25:52.160
But they're the new kind of
effective degrees of freedom

01:25:52.160 --> 01:25:53.860
at that coarse-grain scale.

01:25:53.860 --> 01:25:56.000
And then if you can just end up learning

01:25:56.000 --> 01:25:59.290
like laws on those new
coarse-grain degrees of freedom,

01:25:59.290 --> 01:26:03.130
then you can lift up and
operate very, very quickly,

01:26:03.130 --> 01:26:05.200
compared to the, you know--
- Almost sounds like,

01:26:05.200 --> 01:26:07.390
this is completely alternative.

01:26:07.390 --> 01:26:10.690
It's not quite the demystification
of the standard process

01:26:10.690 --> 01:26:14.320
of discovery, but almost a
brand new way for discovery.

01:26:14.320 --> 01:26:16.500
I mean, at least it feels that way, right?

01:26:16.500 --> 01:26:18.700
- Yeah, it just seems like
there's potentially a path

01:26:18.700 --> 01:26:22.870
to kind of systematically hop between,

01:26:22.870 --> 01:26:24.610
or maybe you already know the right scale

01:26:24.610 --> 01:26:27.180
for whatever reason but you
don't know how they're related.

01:26:27.180 --> 01:26:30.740
And you can simulate, so,
and so there are definitely,

01:26:30.740 --> 01:26:31.747
well, we have some work going on

01:26:31.747 --> 01:26:33.897
that's basically trying
to do exactly that.

01:26:34.809 --> 01:26:36.100
Like, I know how to simulate it one scale.

01:26:36.100 --> 01:26:39.000
I know there's a phenomena
emerge at a different scale,

01:26:39.000 --> 01:26:40.330
so I can predict them.

01:26:40.330 --> 01:26:42.310
And if it doesn't require
like exponential amount

01:26:42.310 --> 01:26:44.770
of computing time, then
I can get enough data

01:26:44.770 --> 01:26:46.977
where I can try to
learn that relationship.

01:26:46.977 --> 01:26:48.877
And that's really compelling, I think.

01:26:49.920 --> 01:26:50.890
- Thanks.

01:26:50.890 --> 01:26:52.817
So Jeremy Zucker has the next question.

01:26:52.817 --> 01:26:55.247
"What do you recommend as
the best Python library

01:26:55.247 --> 01:26:57.657
"for performing
simulation-based inference?"

01:26:58.950 --> 01:27:00.727
- Yeah, so good question.
- And something else,

01:27:00.727 --> 01:27:03.267
"Are there some, any probabilistic
programming languages

01:27:03.267 --> 01:27:05.940
"that can do simulation-based inference?"

01:27:05.940 --> 01:27:08.050
- Yeah, I can give a
quick, quick guide to this

01:27:08.050 --> 01:27:09.350
without getting too into the weeds.

01:27:09.350 --> 01:27:11.270
So that, there's a tool called SBI,

01:27:11.270 --> 01:27:13.230
which stands for
simulation-based inference.

01:27:13.230 --> 01:27:15.630
It's kind of like a nice, general-purpose

01:27:15.630 --> 01:27:18.423
Python wrapper-type of approach.

01:27:19.409 --> 01:27:22.090
So you have a way to kind of interface

01:27:22.090 --> 01:27:24.546
whatever simulator you have to it.

01:27:24.546 --> 01:27:27.130
Generates data, does the
machine learning components,

01:27:27.130 --> 01:27:29.000
and then you can do inference.

01:27:29.000 --> 01:27:32.290
And it implements several
different techniques.

01:27:32.290 --> 01:27:37.137
So LP is kind of, is more tied
to one specific technique.

01:27:38.700 --> 01:27:41.730
And so, yeah, so the, which is fine,

01:27:41.730 --> 01:27:45.020
but it's sort of less
of a toolkit in general.

01:27:45.020 --> 01:27:46.227
And then you mentioned Etalumis.

01:27:46.227 --> 01:27:47.370
- Etalumis, yep.

01:27:47.370 --> 01:27:49.220
- Yeah, so that was a project
that I was involved in.

01:27:49.220 --> 01:27:52.540
So there's a kind of
important distinction there,

01:27:52.540 --> 01:27:54.280
which I didn't get into.

01:27:54.280 --> 01:27:56.640
But if you remember
this diagram that I drew

01:27:56.640 --> 01:27:59.880
that sort of had parameters on the left,

01:27:59.880 --> 01:28:01.190
data on the right, and in the middle,

01:28:01.190 --> 01:28:03.310
there were latent variables.

01:28:03.310 --> 01:28:06.350
What the Etalumis and PI Prob,

01:28:06.350 --> 01:28:09.240
this probabilistic programming approach,

01:28:09.240 --> 01:28:13.030
it doesn't try to sort of
bypass the latent variables

01:28:13.030 --> 01:28:14.700
and go to straight to the parameters.

01:28:14.700 --> 01:28:16.780
It actually gives you the
capability to do inference

01:28:16.780 --> 01:28:18.720
on the latent variables itself.

01:28:18.720 --> 01:28:20.820
But that's a much harder problem.

01:28:20.820 --> 01:28:24.700
It's a much more complicated
thing to try to infer.

01:28:24.700 --> 01:28:27.740
So if you want to try to do
that, that's the tool for you.

01:28:27.740 --> 01:28:30.310
Just know that it's a lot harder.

01:28:30.310 --> 01:28:32.920
So in that Etalumis work,
we like use supercomputers

01:28:32.920 --> 01:28:34.070
and stuff like that.

01:28:34.070 --> 01:28:36.510
In other contexts, that's not required,

01:28:36.510 --> 01:28:38.310
but kind of depends on your problem.

01:28:39.810 --> 01:28:41.200
- Great, thank you.

01:28:41.200 --> 01:28:44.827
So I guess is next
question is Shyam Sunder's.

01:28:44.827 --> 01:28:46.327
"How much of this can be extended

01:28:46.327 --> 01:28:48.607
"to study of social phenomena?"

01:28:50.540 --> 01:28:51.830
- Yeah, it's a good question.

01:28:51.830 --> 01:28:52.663
I mean, I think,

01:28:54.280 --> 01:28:55.163
well, I mean,

01:28:56.720 --> 01:28:59.193
I had this discussion
once before with like,

01:29:00.180 --> 01:29:02.590
a lot of the way that the
simulation-based inference stuff

01:29:02.590 --> 01:29:04.570
is set up, you need to be able

01:29:04.570 --> 01:29:07.840
to generate data conditioned on,

01:29:07.840 --> 01:29:10.410
or given some, I said parameter,

01:29:10.410 --> 01:29:12.810
but it could be given
some whatever you want.

01:29:12.810 --> 01:29:14.630
So you could, for instance,

01:29:14.630 --> 01:29:18.956
look at the text of tweets
given a certain hashtag.

01:29:18.956 --> 01:29:21.360
And that could be like a political hashtag

01:29:21.360 --> 01:29:26.113
or it could be the use of
some emoji or something

01:29:26.113 --> 01:29:27.480
like your favorite sports team

01:29:27.480 --> 01:29:29.210
or something like that, right?

01:29:29.210 --> 01:29:31.320
So it doesn't actually
have to be a simulator.

01:29:31.320 --> 01:29:34.060
You just need a data-generating process

01:29:34.060 --> 01:29:35.880
that you can condition on.

01:29:35.880 --> 01:29:37.050
And so that makes it interesting

01:29:37.050 --> 01:29:38.870
because you have the opportunity

01:29:38.870 --> 01:29:40.490
to realize what I was talking about

01:29:40.490 --> 01:29:43.710
when you have real world
data generation process.

01:29:43.710 --> 01:29:46.820
So also, like for instance,
I showed this toy.

01:29:46.820 --> 01:29:49.580
I can just generate
data from this toy over

01:29:49.580 --> 01:29:51.880
and over and over, it doesn't
have to be a simulator.

01:29:51.880 --> 01:29:55.400
I can use the actual physical
system to generate the data

01:29:55.400 --> 01:29:57.980
and use all the same
methodology that I described.

01:29:57.980 --> 01:30:01.640
So I think there's a, there's
an opportunity to use that

01:30:01.640 --> 01:30:03.760
in all sorts of different
like social contexts

01:30:03.760 --> 01:30:04.840
and things like that.

01:30:04.840 --> 01:30:06.840
Obviously, if you're
gonna go down that road,

01:30:06.840 --> 01:30:09.730
you should take a moment
to think about the ethics

01:30:09.730 --> 01:30:12.660
of what you're doing
and all of the things.

01:30:12.660 --> 01:30:14.300
Those are important considerations.

01:30:14.300 --> 01:30:16.370
But in terms of the methodology itself,

01:30:16.370 --> 01:30:18.883
there's certainly opportunities.

01:30:20.520 --> 01:30:22.457
- Great, thank you very much.

01:30:22.457 --> 01:30:24.587
So the next question is
from Michael Churchill.

01:30:24.587 --> 01:30:26.277
"For simulation-based inference,

01:30:26.277 --> 01:30:28.707
"what are the prospects for working

01:30:28.707 --> 01:30:30.697
"with very, very expensive simulators,

01:30:30.697 --> 01:30:32.750
"like exascale computing?"

01:30:33.960 --> 01:30:36.010
- Yeah, so that's a good,
also a really good question.

01:30:36.010 --> 01:30:39.730
So in the context of
Large Hadron Collider,

01:30:39.730 --> 01:30:41.050
the real collisions are happening

01:30:41.050 --> 01:30:42.860
about 40 million times a second,

01:30:42.860 --> 01:30:46.550
but to simulate one collision
takes about 10 minutes.

01:30:46.550 --> 01:30:47.900
So there's like an enormous

01:30:49.610 --> 01:30:52.010
gap between how long, but
10 minutes doesn't sound

01:30:52.010 --> 01:30:54.860
that bad and you can run it
on a fairly normal computer

01:30:54.860 --> 01:30:56.830
and we have a lot of computers.

01:30:56.830 --> 01:30:59.330
So it's expensive, but it's doable.

01:30:59.330 --> 01:31:02.130
If you wanted to go into a
situation like, for instance,

01:31:03.570 --> 01:31:07.450
you look at the waveforms
that come inside of the LIGO,

01:31:07.450 --> 01:31:08.980
the gravitational-wave detector,

01:31:08.980 --> 01:31:12.320
then you wanna predict what
those waveforms look like,

01:31:12.320 --> 01:31:17.010
given some black hole merger,

01:31:17.010 --> 01:31:19.350
that simulation, at one point,

01:31:19.350 --> 01:31:22.150
requires numerical relativity
and all sorts of stuff.

01:31:22.150 --> 01:31:26.410
Or then there's other phases
where you can do that faster,

01:31:26.410 --> 01:31:27.890
but it's still pretty expensive.

01:31:27.890 --> 01:31:30.523
And that's to simulate the
waveform for like one scenario.

01:31:30.523 --> 01:31:32.120
And now you wanna scan through this

01:31:32.120 --> 01:31:33.770
fairly high-dimensional space

01:31:33.770 --> 01:31:36.370
that describes the different
ways the black holes

01:31:36.370 --> 01:31:37.840
might be oriented and their mass

01:31:37.840 --> 01:31:39.340
and spin and all these things.

01:31:40.270 --> 01:31:42.201
So that becomes expensive.

01:31:42.201 --> 01:31:45.910
And, but actually, if you
wanted to try to do normal,

01:31:45.910 --> 01:31:47.420
like Bayesian inference in that setting,

01:31:47.420 --> 01:31:48.760
it's really, really, really expensive.

01:31:48.760 --> 01:31:50.880
So people have started using

01:31:50.880 --> 01:31:52.210
likelihood-free inference techniques

01:31:52.210 --> 01:31:53.780
or simulation-based inference techniques

01:31:53.780 --> 01:31:55.430
basically to make that faster.

01:31:55.430 --> 01:31:58.090
And so what's nice is that
you now have situations,

01:31:58.090 --> 01:32:00.140
you pay an upfront training cost,

01:32:00.140 --> 01:32:01.490
but once you're done with that, you have

01:32:01.490 --> 01:32:04.180
what's called an amortized setup

01:32:04.180 --> 01:32:06.060
where you can do inference very quickly.

01:32:06.060 --> 01:32:08.160
So now there's systems where they can look

01:32:08.160 --> 01:32:10.810
at the waveforms and within microseconds,

01:32:10.810 --> 01:32:12.850
tell you what the posterior
distribution on the sky is

01:32:12.850 --> 01:32:17.850
so that you can slew your telescopes.

01:32:17.860 --> 01:32:20.890
But there's definitely a lot
of ongoing work about how to--

01:32:20.890 --> 01:32:23.130
- But that, but Kyle, in that case,

01:32:23.130 --> 01:32:25.380
the training set is
finite and limited, right?

01:32:25.380 --> 01:32:28.190
So there's a limited set of waveforms

01:32:28.190 --> 01:32:30.760
that are real gravitational waves, right?

01:32:30.760 --> 01:32:32.213
Or you think that when you say,

01:32:32.213 --> 01:32:33.140
when you refer to the training set,

01:32:33.140 --> 01:32:36.902
do you mean like the full set
of all possible wave patterns?

01:32:36.902 --> 01:32:39.010
- Or like the ones that you,
the ones that you would get

01:32:39.010 --> 01:32:40.673
from like say running a simulation--

01:32:40.673 --> 01:32:42.560
- A GR simulation, yeah, GR.

01:32:42.560 --> 01:32:45.680
- With whatever approximations or things

01:32:45.680 --> 01:32:46.590
you wanna stick in your simulator.

01:32:46.590 --> 01:32:47.850
I don't wanna get into the weeds

01:32:47.850 --> 01:32:49.423
of like post Newtonian stuff.

01:32:52.859 --> 01:32:54.780
But the, what was I gonna say?

01:32:54.780 --> 01:32:57.313
Sorry, the, oh, the other thing there is

01:32:57.313 --> 01:32:59.130
that there's a lot of work going on,

01:32:59.130 --> 01:33:01.280
which is, at the beginning,
say, like for that example,

01:33:01.280 --> 01:33:04.080
you don't know where on
the sky and you don't know,

01:33:04.080 --> 01:33:05.950
have any idea what the
mass of black holes were.

01:33:05.950 --> 01:33:08.500
But you could make a
very rough scan at first

01:33:08.500 --> 01:33:10.930
and start to get an idea and then refine

01:33:10.930 --> 01:33:13.350
with more simulations
in the relevant region.

01:33:13.350 --> 01:33:15.910
And so you have this kind
of iterative approach

01:33:15.910 --> 01:33:19.240
that uses this buzzword of
kind of active learning.

01:33:19.240 --> 01:33:21.710
And so there are techniques

01:33:21.710 --> 01:33:23.610
that do simulation-based inference

01:33:23.610 --> 01:33:24.870
where there's like an active learning loop

01:33:24.870 --> 01:33:28.610
on the top to try to make the simulation

01:33:28.610 --> 01:33:30.590
as efficient as possible.

01:33:30.590 --> 01:33:32.870
- Because, I mean, these
are cases where doing,

01:33:32.870 --> 01:33:35.260
having a library would be too onerous,

01:33:35.260 --> 01:33:37.351
would be too computationally
expensive, right?

01:33:37.351 --> 01:33:39.170
- Yeah, trying to do it upfront
is like, yeah, not great.

01:33:39.170 --> 01:33:41.380
And earlier there's a
question about software.

01:33:41.380 --> 01:33:43.210
This SBI tool, for instance,

01:33:43.210 --> 01:33:45.590
implements these
active-learning techniques,

01:33:45.590 --> 01:33:47.140
but there's some others as well, yeah.

01:33:47.140 --> 01:33:49.490
There's one called Swift,
which is pretty nice.

01:33:51.160 --> 01:33:52.050
- Let me see if there are,

01:33:52.050 --> 01:33:54.710
I think there's one more question.

01:33:54.710 --> 01:33:55.847
It's Jeremy Zucker again,

01:33:55.847 --> 01:33:58.027
"If your simulator is differentiable,

01:33:58.027 --> 01:34:01.567
"what methods exist to speed
up simulation-based inference?"

01:34:02.740 --> 01:34:04.470
- Yeah, that's a great question.

01:34:04.470 --> 01:34:07.760
So I mean, without trying
to give a shameless plug

01:34:07.760 --> 01:34:09.890
or something, we wrote a review article

01:34:09.890 --> 01:34:12.130
about simulation-based inference
techniques, which maybe--

01:34:12.130 --> 01:34:14.413
- It's a great article,
by the way, I can plug it.

01:34:14.413 --> 01:34:16.990
It was in PNAS and I
think it got me started

01:34:16.990 --> 01:34:19.210
thinking about these issues.

01:34:19.210 --> 01:34:22.470
- Yeah, but it was meant
to be fairly accessible.

01:34:22.470 --> 01:34:25.380
One of the things that we spent
some time there is saying,

01:34:25.380 --> 01:34:28.420
if you can open up your
simulator, open up the black box

01:34:28.420 --> 01:34:31.970
and either instrument it with
probabilistic programming

01:34:31.970 --> 01:34:35.190
type of skills, or if it's differentiable,

01:34:35.190 --> 01:34:36.730
there's different ways
it can be differentiable.

01:34:36.730 --> 01:34:37.770
It can be differentiable in the sense

01:34:37.770 --> 01:34:40.040
of like input-output relationships,

01:34:40.040 --> 01:34:41.490
or it can be differentiable in the sense

01:34:41.490 --> 01:34:45.820
of the probability of
this run of the simulator,

01:34:45.820 --> 01:34:48.470
Like what's the derivative
of that probability

01:34:48.470 --> 01:34:50.740
with respect to the
parameters of the simulator?

01:34:50.740 --> 01:34:52.180
So there are different
kinds of derivatives

01:34:52.180 --> 01:34:53.226
that you can take.

01:34:53.226 --> 01:34:56.180
You can take advantage of them

01:34:56.180 --> 01:35:00.250
and it can lead to dramatically
more efficient learning.

01:35:00.250 --> 01:35:03.220
So we have a paper called "Mining Gold"

01:35:03.220 --> 01:35:05.630
which talks about one type of derivative.

01:35:05.630 --> 01:35:08.132
And there, we show that
for several problems

01:35:08.132 --> 01:35:09.190
that we were looking at,

01:35:09.190 --> 01:35:10.540
we could reduce the simulation budget

01:35:10.540 --> 01:35:12.320
by like three orders of magnitude.

01:35:12.320 --> 01:35:14.580
So if you can take those derivatives,

01:35:14.580 --> 01:35:16.860
things that were taking millions,

01:35:16.860 --> 01:35:20.478
tens of millions of simulations
turned into like 1,000.

01:35:20.478 --> 01:35:22.433
So that's a pretty big difference.

01:35:24.020 --> 01:35:26.450
- Great, thanks very much.

01:35:26.450 --> 01:35:28.303
Are there any other questions?

01:35:29.759 --> 01:35:31.680
I'll just give everyone another minute

01:35:31.680 --> 01:35:35.853
if they want to type
something up or type up.

01:35:39.170 --> 01:35:41.920
If not, I mean, to be continued.

01:35:41.920 --> 01:35:45.150
I really look forward to the conversation

01:35:45.150 --> 01:35:47.990
between you and Zenna next Wednesday.

01:35:47.990 --> 01:35:51.150
Meanwhile, thanks Kyle
for this wonderful talk,

01:35:51.150 --> 01:35:52.523
really clear talk,

01:35:53.460 --> 01:35:57.010
explaining simulation-based
inference to us

01:35:57.010 --> 01:36:00.500
